{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff --no-ext-diff --unified=20 ddb770352f35f2f55c740e9a8479686de3a251e5..be42f2eff3ce564af794704083dee0b908abc387 -- POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 061d04fb..c9254a16 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -403,41 +403,41 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - Assessment freshness (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — a component assessment records its binding: the committed tree of the component, the engine version and the validation-matrix digest. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reuses an assessment whose binding is unchanged and refreshes it otherwise, printing `assess.freshness.<component>` with the reason (content changed, engine changed, matrix changed, uncommitted changes, or no binding on an older record). Age alone never stales an assessment. A missing or stale assessment of a component an in-progress spec declares appears as an advisory `evidence` obligation in `state --attention`; it gates nothing.\n - `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lists open follow-ups with a reconciliation reason: `target-terminal` (a spec the follow-up names is done, superseded or abandoned), `evidence-present` (a test it names now exists), `overdue` (its review date passed), plus lexical near-duplicate clusters. Each candidate shows its evidence and its limits; nothing is dispositioned, similarity never becomes `duplicate` without judgment, and an open follow-up never leaves Attention without a disposition written in its spec. Attention shows the same reason on the residual-debt item.\n - `action list --present [--actor <id|role>] [--json]` (spec `pose-action-request-presentation`) — groups the requests still waiting for an answer for one conversation: by origin and earliest restricted phase, the ones restricting start or execution first (`interrupt`), release-only ones marked as able to wait. Each request keeps its own id, request digest, revision and answer, and shows exactly the content a resolution binds to; satisfied requests are not asked again. MCP: `pose_action_requests` with `present: true`. Deciding when to interrupt a person is left to the consumer (Harne8).\n - Progressive spec surface (spec `pose-progressive-spec-surface`) — `pose new-spec <slug> --surface minimal|standard|full` records `surface:` in the frontmatter. A `minimal` spec keeps Intent, Requirements, the Technical Plan with its Artifacts, Validation with the requirement trace and the Final Report with its follow-ups; it drops the planner-local Tasks section and the report subsections that `pose specs facts <slug> [--json]` derives from the delivery index (paths of the attributed commits, checks that produced scoped evidence). `lint-spec` stops requiring Tasks for a minimal spec and relaxes nothing else: the trace, follow-up and amendment gates apply as before. The facts never include intent, rationale or accepted risk. Evidence binding (spec `pose-spec-facts-evidence-binding`): `specs facts` separates `checks` (results whose scope provenance names the spec, with their basis and whether they ran against the head of the spec's change sets) from `candidates` (results the index holds that nothing ties to the spec: a declared module or an empty `components` is not provenance); candidates are printed as `CANDIDATE, not evidence of this spec`. `freshness` reports `fresh`, `stale` (an input changed since the index was published), `unknown` (never published as a set) or `unavailable`, and a limitation says to run `pose index` when it cannot be established; reading the facts never rebuilds the index and never concludes that a requirement is satisfied.\n - **Automatic project-state refresh** (spec `pose-project-state-refresh-contract`) — an internal post-event hook registry fires a **partial** `pose state refresh` (only the sections an event affects) at the points POSE already intercepts: a successful `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict` on a `status: done` spec (`spec_closeout` → Specs & Roadmaps, Follow-ups, Validation & Evidence), `pose amend` (`spec_amend` → Specs & Roadmaps, Decisions & Knowledge), `pose reconcile-evidence record` (`evidence_reconciled` → Validation & Evidence) and `pose assess snapshot` (`assessment_snapshot` → Capabilities). When the event carries a commit and `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured, the consumer calls `components_hit` (spec `graphforge-components-hit-contract`) from the state's baseline to the event's commit and attaches the hit components to the Architecture section (**directed** refresh); without GraphForge configured, refresh stays full/undirected — zero build coupling. No daemon, no filesystem watcher: it is a synchronous call at the exact point each event already happens. **Best-effort by default**: a failed refresh never blocks the command that fired the event — it marks `refresh_pending: <event>` in the state frontmatter instead (cleared by the next successful refresh, regardless of trigger). Strict mode is opt-in via policy (`strict_refresh: true`) — there, a refresh failure fails the triggering command. Every run (triggered or manual) is logged to the artifact's own append-only log (metadata only: trigger, target, result `ok|failed|skipped`, duration, changed-section hashes — never content). Dedup key `hash(event+target+commit)`: the same event processed twice (retry/replay) does not repeat the refresh, result `skipped`; `manual`/`ci` refreshes are never deduped against each other (an explicit call always runs). `release_cut` is registered in the event→sections map but has no producer inside pose-mcp today — cutting a release belongs to Conductor (a separate service); the trigger is ready for when that integration exists.\n - **Capability reassessment triggers** (spec `pose-capability-assessment-triggers`, same hook registry as the automatic project-state refresh above) — the `assessment-staleness` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components a closeout reached (via `components_hit` when `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured; fallback: intersecting the event's touched files against each mechanism's manually declared `paths:` globs in the assessment) and marks every affected capability mechanism stale — never mutating a score, only recording `since`/`trigger`/`hits` and projecting a cobrável demand (origin `assessment-trigger`) into `pose followups --open`, with no duplicate storage. `pose assess snapshot` clears the marks on the mechanisms it reassesses and records the mark→snapshot link in the history. On-demand: `pose assess stale [--json]` lists pending marks; `pose assess request --mechanism <id> [--reason <text>]` creates one manually (the same path a UI-driven \"flag for reassessment\" action would call over MCP). MCP tool: `pose_capability_stale`. Without GraphForge and without `paths:` declared, the event logs `capability_mapping_unavailable` to the refresh log — a visible signal, nothing marked silently. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level` `direct`/`any`, the demand's default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy file already used for age/commit-based staleness (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\n - **Docs governance** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — an opt-in, per-project contract for documentation, same mechanic as the capability assessment above: absent, every project stays valid everywhere. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` scaffolds a manifest declaring governed doc roots and, per doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, or a custom value)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/optional `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` runs seven deterministic, offline rules — a declared doc missing from disk, a file present but undeclared, missing minimal frontmatter (`title`/`doc_type`), a broken relative link, a broken typed reference (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (an entry's own review date, or the manifest's default review window counted from its last touching commit) and a security scan reusing the same deterministic unsafe-instruction/secret-shaped pattern check skills already run — each with a configurable severity (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorporates `docs-check` when the manifest exists (opt-in by presence); only errors block, warnings surface without blocking. `pose docs-sync` synchronizes governance metadata. Tool MCP: `pose_docs_state`. Project-state gains an additive Docs section (manifest presence/profile/roots, declared/undeclared/stale/error/warning counts).\n - **Docs review-pending triggers** (spec `pose-docs-assessment-followups`, third consumer of the same hook registry as the two entries above — reused unmodified) — a `docs-review` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components/files a closeout reached (`components_hit` when configured, matched against `owns:` entries declared as `component:<id>`; otherwise the event's touched files matched against each doc's `owns:` paths/globs, where a directory like `\"site\"` covers every file under it) and marks every doc whose declared area was reached as review-pending — never editing the doc itself, never touching a score. Marks accumulate in an append-only log outside the doc's own file and project a synthetic, owned demand into `pose followups --open` (origin `docs:<doc-path>`), reusing the manifest entry's own `owner` field when declared. `pose docs-review resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]` closes every mark pending on a doc at once, recording `updated` (default, captures the current commit) or `no_change_needed` (reason required); `pose docs-review request <doc>`/`--all-stale` cover the on-demand path — the latter bridges every currently-stale doc into an active demand in one call. `docs-check`'s own output and `pose_docs_state` additively list what's still pending. Without a component map and without `owns:` declared, the event logs a visible signal instead of marking anything silently — here the path fallback is the mechanism's full-strength path (`owns:` is expressed as paths by default), not a lesser one. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level`, default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy shape as the capability triggers in its own file (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\n - `history-check` — verifies every `.jsonl` under `reports/history/` is git-tracked. Without that, `recurrence-check` and `stats` diverge between machines. Strict blocks; tolerant warns.\n - `suggest` — reads [`task-map.json`](.pose/indexes/task-map.json) and prints the canonical trail (workflow + skill + rules + spec/ADR + knowledge) for a task type. With no arguments it lists every type. `--domain <d>` adds domain rules (frontend, backend-go, k8s); `--path <p>` infers the domain heuristically and via [`repo-map.json`](.pose/indexes/repo-map.json) (`language` → frontend/backend-go); `--json` for agents. Domains (spec `pose-distributed-instructions-parity`): `--domain` and the domain inferred from `--path` go through one taxonomy (`go`/`golang`/`backend-go` → `backend-go`; `react`/`frontend-react`/`frontend` → `frontend`; `kubernetes`/`helm`/`k8s` → `k8s`), the JSON discloses `domain_input` and `domain_source: alias`, and a domain the task map has no rules for is reported (`domain_status`) instead of borrowing another's; a `backend` module is the Go domain only when its language is Go. `rules_status` (and the human output) says for each rule whether it is `installed` (with its path), `extension-needed` (with `pose extension install <id>`) or `unavailable`; suggest announces no rule file that is not on disk and installs nothing.\n - `stats` — aggregates history JSONL outcomes by workflow, task or context. Enables objective decisions (promoting a check from optional to required, spotting unstable workflows, comparing ci against manual). `--since-days N` filters the window; `--json` for machine consumption.\n - `stats governance` — schema 2 read-only projection over report history, sealed review bundles, attestations and explicit remediation declarations. It keeps preparation, judgment, intervention, freshness, telemetry, provenance and coverage independent, never emits a quality score, and reports missing/invalid data explicitly. `--report-type` filters validation attempts; `--band` filters reviews and finding links. Replay identity is `(report_type, task_slug, sequence)`; identity-less rows remain visible in coverage. MCP `pose_governance_stats` accepts the same selectors and requires `project_id` when choosing a federated root. Explicit finding links contain only opaque IDs, spec slugs, category and source band, with at most 100 rows and truncation reported.\n - `stats governance --waits --rework` (spec `pose-governance-wait-rework-observability`) — two more dimensions beside the outcomes, never folded into a score. With `--waits` or `--rework` only the requested dimensions are computed; `--outcomes` adds the outcomes, which read the whole history. `--waits` times action requests and keeps three measures apart: the age of each request, the interval attributed to waiting on an actor or an external system, and `known_blocking_seconds`, the union per spec of intervals in which a request restricted the whole spec's execution (simultaneous requests count once). None of them is inactivity; missing timestamps are counted as unknown. `--rework` classifies superseded sealed bundles by what changed (subject, intent or plan, review plan, evidence, or unknown) and extra attestations on one bundle by review disagreement or an earlier attestation invalid against today's policy, leaving other causes unknown. `scripts/bench-governance.sh [runs] [pose-binary]` times `state --attention`, `check --strict` and `index` on the same revision and reports time and the index size separately, with the corpus revision and engine version.\n - Premise validity (spec `pose-assumption-validity-scope`) — a material `Assumption A<N>` may declare `Valid scope:` (the context it holds in) and `Stale trigger:` entries `<kind>:<value>@<pin>`, where kind is `doc`, `report`, `knowledge`, `adr` or `snapshot` and the pin is a prefix (8+ hex) of the sha256 of the content the premise was judged against. `lint-spec --design-check` reports an unpinned trigger with the pin to append, an unreadable one as unknown, and a calendar trigger (`date:`, `ttl:`, …) as not evaluated: validity is by context, not expiry, and trivial premises need neither field. When pinned content changes, the obligation projection (producer `premises`) emits a `premise-stale` judgment obligation for draft and in-progress specs, advisory on review and scoped to the assumption and the decisions based on it. The recorded evidence stays; it is described as judged against the pinned content and not valid for the current one, and nothing is invalidated automatically. Provider or remote contracts are not monitored by the core. Trigger state is projection only and never changes the design digest.\n - Falsifier observation (spec `pose-falsifier-reconsideration`) — a material `Decision D<N>` may add `Expected effect:` and `Falsifier check: check:<module>/<check>`, naming the indexed validation check whose failure contradicts it. When the delivery index holds a `fail` result for that check, the obligation projection (producer `falsifiers`) emits a `falsifier-observed` judgment obligation, advisory on review and scoped to the decision, for any spec that is not superseded or abandoned (decisions stay in force after done). The engine infers no cause and judges no architecture: the failure is an observation, the decision and its rationale are never edited, and a person either records why it still holds or opens a superseding decision. A passing or absent result raises nothing. `lint-spec --design-check` warns on a malformed reference and on a falsifier check without a stated falsifier.\n - `migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]` (spec `pose-v7-legacy-cleanup-plan`) — read-only inventory of what a future major would convert, keep or refuse: spec layouts, `blocked` specs, legacy and unknown review policy keys, sealed bundles, attestations by identity mode and attribution, and incomplete transfers on this project's side, each with its action and removal criterion. It writes nothing. Sealed bundles, attestations and signatures are never rewritten. `--apply` is refused with the current fact: no apply is available in this engine, the only mode is `--dry-run`, which writes nothing; no future apply is promised.\n - `assess design --spec <slug>` — read-only bounded projection over the canonical sealed review subject. It observes dependency, component, delivery-metadata, governance-contract and public-contract deltas, keeps unknown/unsupported coverage explicit, and never computes a complexity or architecture score. The MCP equivalent is `pose_design_delta`.\n - `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\n - `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\n - `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\n - `knowledge-usage` — projects `knowledge:<slug>` citations from specs per artifact (owner, expiry, citing specs). Usage signals inform the owner's review; TTL is never extended automatically. Dangling `knowledge:` refs fail `knowledge-check`.\n - `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — deterministic lexical ranking and advisory projections for knowledge, feedback clustering, multi-roadmap portfolio milestones, and delivery validation evidence reconciliation. Suggestions never gate or auto-apply without confirmation.\n - `hooks` — links the native binary into `.git/hooks/`; invocation name selects `check --tolerant` for `pre-commit` and `index` for `post-merge`. `install --force` keeps a backup of pre-existing hooks. An instance that runs POSE through its own revision-bound CLI declares a runner in a `hooks.json` file of `.pose/policy/` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"runner\":\"<project-relative executable>\"}`): the hook then runs the runner with the hook's name and returns its exit code, and `hooks status` names it; a runner outside the project, not executable or under another schema is refused and nothing runs.\n - `version` — displays compiled Go binary version, commit SHA, and compares the repository schema version with engine requirements.\n - `context` — resolves a selected logical project and optional local or `xref:`-qualified task through the shared artifact resolver. The JSON result includes canonical authority, project/artifact revisions, resolution state, supported contracts and a `context_revision`; it never includes filesystem roots. Use this token to detect a changed checkout or revision before a cross-project write.\n - Cross-project CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the `context_revision` from a fresh `pose context` call. Use `new-spec-qualified` for qualified creation so older engines reject the command itself. A project found only by `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` scan can be read but does not grant write access. Existing canonical specs are reused; redirects and incomplete transfers block shadow creation.\n - `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — installs the embedded POSE runtime, rules, workflows, templates, and documentation into a target project directory without cloning, stamps `adopted_at` in the changelog policy with the day the instance received it, and runs the strict gate.\n - `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — imports foreign specification trees into canonical POSE specifications with standard frontmatter and section structure.\n - `serve-mcp` — starts the POSE Model Context Protocol server over stdio (`--stdio`) or HTTP for native AI agent integration. The stdio server exits on SIGTERM; it resolves the `pose` CLI it runs once, at start, so a server started before `pose update` keeps working with the updated binary.\n - `report-limitation` — records an engine limitation, defect, or enhancement proposal into `.pose/feedback/` with optional submission (`--submit`).\n - `contribute <enable|disable|status|stage|list>` — open-source contributor mode; stages sanitized reports under `.pose/contributions/` without leaking proprietary code; submission remains developer-adjudicated.\n - `telemetry <enable|disable|status>` — manages anonymous, opt-in platform telemetry settings.\n - `release` / `release-notes` — governed release lifecycle (`plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`, `status`, `open-next`, `backfill`) ensuring immutable release notes, manifest freezing, provider evidence importing, and reproducible release verification. A release that introduces a governance contract carries a **Compatibility** section at the top of its notes, generated from the contract registry. `release-notes` provides a compatibility alias for viewing frozen notes. The version a cut is compared with comes from `version_source` in `.pose/policy/release.json` (`{\"path\": \"VERSION\", \"kind\": \"text\"}`, or `\"kind\": \"json\"` with a `key`): a project file holding `X.Y.Z`, with an optional `v` and `-dev` suffix. Only the engine repository may omit it; every other project must declare one. Manifest and tag integrity (spec `pose-release-manifest-tag-integrity`): a manifest is read through a strict contract (supported `schema_version`, no unknown fields, clean version and paths) that recomposes `specs`, `categories`, `breaking` and `release_input_digest` from the fragments it freezes, so an adulterated projection is refused instead of normalised. `check` and `status` verify a tag from its own tree: the tag (lightweight or annotated) is peeled to its commit, and the manifest, notes, frozen fragments and version source are read as blobs of that commit, never from the working tree. A tag whose tree holds no manifest is refused whatever the working tree holds; a later commit, provider evidence or the next cycle does not make a correct cut stale; a version source that cannot be recomputed from the manifest is reported as a limit.\n \n   **Query cursors:** paginated MCP list tools (`pose_list_specs`, `pose_list_roadmaps`, `pose_list_knowledge`, `pose_list_reports`, `pose_capability_history`) continue a query over the collection it was issued for. A cursor is signed, expires after 15 minutes and is bound to its tool, project, filters and the ordered collection with its index generation; a changed collection, another query, an expired token, a version 1 token or a server restart is refused with a `cursor-*` code and the caller restarts from the first page, never continued over a changed collection. A page is at most 500 items, discloses its `generation`, `collection` and `total`, and a missing `next_cursor` does not certify anything beyond that collection (spec `pose-generation-bound-query-cursors`).\n \n@@ -1054,29 +1054,31 @@ Declare the executor and implementation baseline before dispatch. A bundle that\n \n ```json\n {\n   \"schema_version\": 1,\n   \"provenance\": {\n     \"trust\": \"executor-attested\",\n     \"implementation\": {\"principal\": \"agent:implementer\", \"execution\": \"implementation-run-1\", \"vendor\": \"vendor-a\", \"model\": \"model-a\"}\n   },\n   \"adapters\": {\n     \"reviewer\": {\"executor\": \"agent:reviewer\", \"command\": [\"reviewer\", \"--output\", \"{output}\"], \"vendor\": \"vendor-b\", \"model\": \"model-b\"}\n   }\n }\n ```\n \n Use the existing native issuer and its attestation pin. Set protected `authority_project` and `authority_audience` in review policy; a declared project identity must agree. Keep keys outside the project. Dispatch captures origin/worktree, bundle/commit/scope/kind, configuration/argv/executable, identities, brief template/body/notes, planned stdin and actual pipe-write digests, raw and retained redacted output/transcript digests and completeness. A pipe write does not prove the vendor consumed the prompt. Session references and private keys stay outside the public proof.\n \n Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then add `--apply` to publish an immutable proof under `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL is optional (no expiry when absent), positive when supplied and at most 365 days. Verify it with `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. The proof signs the captured executor declaration; it is not an independent vendor receipt or human confirmation. Failed or interrupted audit records disclose their state and `complete_review=false`. Unknown or ambiguous draft decisions also keep that flag false. A true flag describes completed delivery with one draft decision, not approval or complete typed-conclusion validation. A completed run with incomplete stdin or truncated output cannot establish a completed review. Legacy runs have no captured provenance and cannot be upgraded by signing today's configuration. `pose review run-proof` and the issuer lookups of the verdict readers ask the question the closeout asks: for a run whose scope is done, the proof as it was given (the record stands, the issuer still holds its pin; routine drift of the repository and the proof's expiry are not asked, because the expiry bounds when a proof may back a new attestation, not whether an attestation already given to a done scope stands); for an open scope, as the repository is now.\n \n Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n \n-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/8` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/8` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n \n-**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+\n+**Runs that leave work behind.** After the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own, waits for them, and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. A dispatch that ended between reserving its attempt and recording its run leaves the attempt stalled and every later dispatch of the bundle refused; `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt.\n \n **Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n \n Opt in to the verification bar with `delegated_review: true` in review policy only when preparing adoption. This live bar requires a signed run reference for agent different-actor review and refuses a prefix-only identity; it does not enable or add the future catalog capability. Non-adopting instances keep declared behavior. Under explicit executor-attested trust, normalized vendor OR model difference satisfies different-actor (vendor difference preferred); unknown baselines and the same vendor/model refuse. A machine run never satisfies mandatory-human. Catalog adoption, cutoffs/rollback and automatic conclusion application remain separate capability work.\n \n Read the same current assurance through verify, review-check, MCP and `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` names the signed executor declaration, its source, run/proof and vendor/model comparisons. Current issuer pins, project/audience/time, sealed inputs, retained artifacts and original/continuation receipts are reverified. Revocation, missing receipts, rewritten data or stale inputs remove machine assurance. Signing does not reset quotas or erase earlier rejecting runs. The origin worktree is recorded with the run, not required of the reader: a proof verifies in any checkout of the project, so a run a CI job or the Conductor performed is verified like a local one; no universal containment is claimed.\ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 1f7e93c1..efb29ab8 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -407,41 +407,41 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - Frescor de assessment (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — um assessment de componente registra seu vínculo: a árvore commitada do componente, a versão do motor e o digest da matriz de validação. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reaproveita um assessment cujo vínculo não mudou e o refaz caso contrário, mostrando `assess.freshness.<componente>` com o motivo (conteúdo mudou, motor mudou, matriz mudou, mudanças não commitadas, ou registro antigo sem vínculo). Idade sozinha nunca torna um assessment stale. Um assessment ausente ou stale de um componente declarado por uma spec em andamento aparece como obrigação consultiva de `evidence` em `state --attention`; não bloqueia nada.\n - `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lista follow-ups abertos com um motivo de reconciliação: `target-terminal` (uma spec citada pelo follow-up está done, superseded ou abandoned), `evidence-present` (um teste citado agora existe), `overdue` (a data de revisão passou), além de clusters lexicais de quase-duplicatas. Cada candidato mostra sua evidência e seus limites; nada recebe disposição, similaridade nunca vira `duplicate` sem julgamento, e um follow-up aberto nunca sai do Attention sem uma disposição escrita na spec. O Attention mostra o mesmo motivo no item de dívida residual.\n - `action list --present [--actor <id|papel>] [--json]` (spec `pose-action-request-presentation`) — agrupa as solicitações que ainda esperam resposta para uma única conversa: por origem e primeira fase restringida, as que restringem start ou execution primeiro (`interrupt`), as que restringem só a release marcadas como capazes de esperar. Cada solicitação mantém seu id, digest, revisão e resposta, e mostra exatamente o conteúdo ao qual a resolução se vincula; solicitações satisfeitas não são perguntadas de novo. MCP: `pose_action_requests` com `present: true`. Decidir quando interromper uma pessoa fica com o consumidor (Harne8).\n - Superfície progressiva de spec (spec `pose-progressive-spec-surface`) — `pose new-spec <slug> --surface minimal|standard|full` registra `surface:` no frontmatter. Uma spec `minimal` mantém Intent, Requirements, o Technical Plan com Artifacts, Validation com o requirement trace e o Final Report com os follow-ups; remove a seção Tasks (local do planner) e as subseções do relatório que `pose specs facts <slug> [--json]` deriva do índice de entrega (paths dos commits atribuídos, checks que produziram evidência no escopo). `lint-spec` deixa de exigir Tasks numa spec mínima e não relaxa mais nada: os gates de trace, follow-ups e amendments continuam iguais. Os fatos nunca incluem intenção, rationale ou risco aceito. Vínculo de evidência (spec `pose-spec-facts-evidence-binding`): `specs facts` separa `checks` (resultados cujo scope provenance nomeia a spec, com a base e se rodaram contra o head dos change sets da spec) de `candidates` (resultados que o índice guarda e que nada liga à spec: módulo declarado ou `components` vazio não é provenance); candidatos são impressos como `CANDIDATE, not evidence of this spec`. `freshness` informa `fresh`, `stale` (um input mudou depois da publicação do índice), `unknown` (nunca publicado como conjunto) ou `unavailable`, e uma limitação manda rodar `pose index` quando não dá para estabelecer; ler os facts nunca reconstrói o índice nem conclui que um requisito está satisfeito.\n - **Refresh automático de project-state** (spec `pose-project-state-refresh-contract`) — um registro interno de hooks pós-evento dispara um `pose state refresh` **parcial** (apenas as seções que o evento afeta) nos pontos que o POSE já intercepta: um `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict` bem-sucedido numa spec `status: done` (`spec_closeout` → Specs & Roadmaps, Follow-ups, Validação & Evidência), `pose amend` (`spec_amend` → Specs & Roadmaps, Decisões & Conhecimento), `pose reconcile-evidence record` (`evidence_reconciled` → Validação & Evidência) e `pose assess snapshot` (`assessment_snapshot` → Capabilities). Quando o evento carrega um commit e `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` está configurado, o consumidor chama `components_hit` (spec `graphforge-components-hit-contract`) do baseline do estado até o commit do evento e anexa os componentes atingidos à seção Arquitetura (refresh **dirigido**); sem GraphForge configurado, o refresh permanece completo/não dirigido — zero acoplamento de build. Sem daemon e sem watcher de filesystem: é uma chamada síncrona no ponto exato em que cada evento já acontece. **Best-effort por padrão**: um refresh que falha nunca bloqueia o comando que disparou o evento — em vez disso marca `refresh_pending: <event>` no frontmatter do estado (limpo pelo próximo refresh bem-sucedido, qualquer que seja o gatilho). O modo estrito é opt-in por política (`strict_refresh: true`) — ali, uma falha de refresh falha o comando disparador. Toda execução (disparada ou manual) é registrada no log append-only do próprio artefato (apenas metadados: gatilho, alvo, resultado `ok|failed|skipped`, duração, hashes das seções alteradas — nunca conteúdo). Chave de dedup `hash(event+target+commit)`: o mesmo evento processado duas vezes (retry/replay) não repete o refresh, resultado `skipped`; refreshes `manual`/`ci` nunca são deduplicados entre si (uma chamada explícita sempre roda). `release_cut` está registrado no mapa evento→seções mas não tem produtor dentro do pose-mcp hoje — cortar um release pertence ao Conductor (serviço separado); o gatilho está pronto para quando essa integração existir.\n - **Gatilhos de reavaliação de capability** (spec `pose-capability-assessment-triggers`, mesmo registro de hooks do refresh automático acima) — o consumidor `assessment-staleness`, registrado em `spec_closeout`, resolve quais componentes um closeout alcançou (via `components_hit` quando `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` está configurado; fallback: interseção dos arquivos tocados pelo evento com os globs `paths:` declarados manualmente por mecanismo no assessment) e marca como stale todo mecanismo de capability afetado — nunca mutando um score, apenas registrando `since`/`trigger`/`hits` e projetando uma demanda cobrável (origem `assessment-trigger`) em `pose followups --open`, sem armazenamento duplicado. `pose assess snapshot` limpa as marcas dos mecanismos que reavalia e registra o vínculo marca→snapshot no histórico. Sob demanda: `pose assess stale [--json]` lista marcas pendentes; `pose assess request --mechanism <id> [--reason <text>]` cria uma manualmente (o mesmo caminho que uma ação de UI \"sinalizar para reavaliação\" chamaria via MCP). Ferramenta MCP: `pose_capability_stale`. Sem GraphForge e sem `paths:` declarado, o evento registra `capability_mapping_unavailable` no log de refresh — sinal visível, nada marcado em silêncio. O limiar antirruído (`min_hits`, `level` de hit `direct`/`any`, owner/SLA default da demanda) é configurável, compartilhando o mesmo arquivo de política já usado para staleness por idade/commits (opcional; ausente significa estes mesmos defaults conservadores).\n - **Governança de docs** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — contrato opt-in por projeto para documentação, mesma mecânica do assessment de capability acima: ausente, todo projeto segue válido em todo lugar. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` cria um manifesto declarando as raízes governadas de documentação e, por doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, ou valor customizado)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/opcionalmente `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` roda sete regras determinísticas e offline — doc declarado ausente do disco, arquivo presente mas não declarado, frontmatter mínimo faltando (`title`/`doc_type`), link relativo quebrado, referência tipada quebrada (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (data de review da própria entrada, ou a janela default do manifesto contada a partir do último commit que a tocou) e uma varredura de segurança reusando a mesma checagem determinística de instrução insegura/forma de segredo que as skills já rodam — cada uma com severidade configurável (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorpora o `docs-check` quando o manifesto existe (opt-in por presença); apenas erros bloqueiam, avisos aparecem sem bloquear. `pose docs-sync` sincroniza metadados de governança. Ferramenta MCP: `pose_docs_state`. O project-state ganha uma seção Docs aditiva (presença/perfil/raízes do manifesto, contagens de declarados/não declarados/stale/erro/aviso).\n - **Gatilhos de review pendente de docs** (spec `pose-docs-assessment-followups`, terceiro consumidor do mesmo registro de hooks das duas entradas acima — reusado sem modificação) — um consumidor `docs-review`, registrado em `spec_closeout`, resolve quais componentes/arquivos um closeout alcançou (`components_hit` quando configurado, casado contra entradas `owns:` declaradas como `component:<id>`; caso contrário, os arquivos tocados pelo evento casados contra os paths/globs `owns:` de cada doc, onde um diretório como `\"site\"` cobre todo arquivo abaixo dele) e marca como review-pending todo doc cuja área declarada foi alcançada — nunca editando o doc, nunca tocando um score. As marcas se acumulam num log append-only fora do arquivo do próprio doc e projetam uma demanda sintética e com dono em `pose followups --open` (origem `docs:<caminho-do-doc>`), reusando o campo `owner` da própria entrada do manifesto quando declarado. `pose docs-review resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]` fecha de uma vez todas as marcas pendentes num doc, registrando `updated` (default, captura o commit atual) ou `no_change_needed` (motivo obrigatório); `pose docs-review request <doc>`/`--all-stale` cobrem o caminho sob demanda — o segundo transforma todo doc atualmente stale em demanda ativa numa chamada. A saída do próprio `docs-check` e o `pose_docs_state` listam aditivamente o que segue pendente. Sem mapa de componentes e sem `owns:` declarado, o evento registra um sinal visível em vez de marcar algo em silêncio — aqui o fallback por path é o caminho de força total do mecanismo (`owns:` é expresso como paths por padrão), não um caminho menor. O limiar antirruído (`min_hits`, `level` de hit, owner/SLA default) é configurável, compartilhando o mesmo formato de política dos gatilhos de capability em arquivo próprio (opcional; ausente significa estes mesmos defaults conservadores).\n - `history-check` — verifica que todo `.jsonl` em `reports/history/` está sob versionamento git. Sem isso, `recurrence-check` e `stats` divergem entre máquinas. Strict bloqueia; tolerant avisa.\n - `suggest` — lê [`task-map.json`](.pose/indexes/task-map.json) e imprime a trilha canônica (workflow + skill + rules + spec/ADR + knowledge) para um tipo de tarefa. Sem argumentos, lista todos os tipos. `--domain <d>` aplica rules adicionais por domínio (frontend, backend-go, k8s); `--path <p>` infere o domínio por heurísticas e via [`repo-map.json`](.pose/indexes/repo-map.json) (`language` → frontend/backend-go); `--json` para consumo por agentes. Domínios (spec `pose-distributed-instructions-parity`): `--domain` e o domínio inferido de `--path` passam por uma taxonomia (`go`/`golang`/`backend-go` → `backend-go`; `react`/`frontend-react`/`frontend` → `frontend`; `kubernetes`/`helm`/`k8s` → `k8s`), o JSON expõe `domain_input` e `domain_source: alias`, e um domínio sem regras no task-map é reportado (`domain_status`) em vez de tomar emprestado o de outro; um módulo `backend` só é o domínio Go quando sua linguagem é Go. `rules_status` (e a saída humana) diz de cada regra se está `installed` (com o path), `extension-needed` (com `pose extension install <id>`) ou `unavailable`; suggest não anuncia arquivo de regra que não está no disco e não instala nada.\n - `stats` — agrega outcomes do history JSONL por workflow, task ou context. Habilita decisões objetivas (promover check de optional → required, identificar workflows instáveis, comparar ci vs manual). `--since-days N` filtra a janela; `--json` para consumo por máquina.\n - `stats governance` — projeção read-only schema 2 sobre histórico de reports, bundles de review selados, atestações e declarações explícitas de remediação. Mantém preparação, julgamento, intervenção, frescor, telemetria, proveniência e cobertura independentes, nunca emite score de qualidade e explicita dados ausentes ou inválidos. `--report-type` filtra tentativas de validação; `--band` filtra reviews e vínculos de findings. A identidade de replay é `(report_type, task_slug, sequence)`; linhas sem identidade permanecem visíveis na cobertura. MCP `pose_governance_stats` aceita os mesmos seletores e exige `project_id` para escolher uma raiz federada. Vínculos explícitos incluem apenas IDs opacos, slugs, categoria e banda de origem, limitados a 100 linhas com truncamento indicado.\n - `stats governance --waits --rework` (spec `pose-governance-wait-rework-observability`) — duas dimensões a mais ao lado dos resultados, nunca somadas num score. Com `--waits` ou `--rework`, só as dimensões pedidas são calculadas; `--outcomes` acrescenta os resultados, que leem todo o histórico. `--waits` cronometra as action requests e mantém três medidas separadas: a idade de cada solicitação, o intervalo atribuído à espera por um ator ou sistema externo, e `known_blocking_seconds`, a união por spec dos intervalos em que uma solicitação restringiu a execução da spec inteira (solicitações simultâneas contam uma vez). Nenhuma delas é inatividade; timestamps ausentes contam como desconhecidos. `--rework` classifica bundles selados substituídos pelo que mudou (sujeito, intenção ou plano, plano de review, evidência, ou desconhecido) e atestações extras num bundle por divergência de review ou atestação anterior inválida contra a política atual, deixando outras causas como desconhecidas. `scripts/bench-governance.sh [runs] [pose-binary]` cronometra `state --attention`, `check --strict` e `index` na mesma revisão e reporta tempo e tamanho do índice separadamente, com a revisão do corpus e a versão do motor.\n - Validade de premissas (spec `pose-assumption-validity-scope`) — uma `Premissa A<N>` material pode declarar `Escopo de validade:` (o contexto em que vale) e entradas `Gatilho de obsolescência:` `<kind>:<value>@<pin>`, onde kind é `doc`, `report`, `knowledge`, `adr` ou `snapshot` e o pin é um prefixo (8+ hex) do sha256 do conteúdo contra o qual a premissa foi julgada. `lint-spec --design-check` reporta um gatilho sem pin com o pin a acrescentar, um ilegível como desconhecido, e um gatilho de calendário (`date:`, `ttl:`, …) como não avaliado: validade é por contexto, não por expiração, e premissas triviais não precisam de nenhum campo. Quando o conteúdo fixado muda, a projeção de obrigações (produtor `premises`) emite uma obrigação de julgamento `premise-stale` para specs draft e in-progress, advisory no review e com escopo na premissa e nas decisões baseadas nela. A evidência registrada permanece; é descrita como julgada contra o conteúdo fixado e não válida para o atual, e nada é invalidado automaticamente. Contratos de provedores ou remotos não são monitorados pelo core. O estado do gatilho é só projeção e nunca muda o digest do design.\n - Observação de falsificador (spec `pose-falsifier-reconsideration`) — uma `Decisão D<N>` material pode acrescentar `Efeito esperado:` e `Verificação do falsificador: check:<module>/<check>`, nomeando o check de validação indexado cuja falha a contradiz. Quando o índice de entrega contém um resultado `fail` desse check, a projeção de obrigações (produtor `falsifiers`) emite uma obrigação de julgamento `falsifier-observed`, advisory no review e com escopo na decisão, para qualquer spec que não esteja superseded ou abandoned (decisões continuam valendo depois de done). O motor não infere causa nem julga arquitetura: a falha é uma observação, a decisão e seu rationale nunca são editados, e uma pessoa registra por que ela ainda vale ou abre uma decisão substituta. Um resultado aprovado ou ausente não gera nada. `lint-spec --design-check` avisa sobre referência malformada e sobre verificação de falsificador sem falsificador declarado.\n - `migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]` (spec `pose-v7-legacy-cleanup-plan`) — inventário somente leitura do que uma futura major converteria, manteria ou recusaria: layouts de spec, specs `blocked`, chaves legadas e desconhecidas da política de review, bundles selados, atestações por modo de identidade e atribuição, e transferências incompletas do lado deste projeto, cada um com sua ação e critério de remoção. Não escreve nada. Bundles selados, atestações e assinaturas nunca são reescritos. `--apply` é recusado com o fato atual: não há apply neste motor, o único modo é `--dry-run`, que não escreve nada; nenhum apply futuro é prometido.\n - `assess design --spec <slug>` — projeção read-only e bounded sobre o subject canônico selado de review. Observa deltas de dependência, componente, metadata de delivery, contratos de governança e contratos públicos, mantém coverage unknown/unsupported explícita e nunca calcula score de complexidade ou arquitetura. O equivalente MCP é `pose_design_delta`.\n - `usage` — agrega automaticamente eventos locais e por projeto de uso da CLI e do MCP: chamadas, outcomes de execução/semântica, latência, findings observados e ciclo de vida estável (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agentes nunca mantêm contadores. Os eventos são best-effort, somente locais e ficam fora da árvore versionada; argumentos, saída, paths, identidade de projeto/usuário e IDs crus de findings nunca são persistidos. `--since-days 0` inclui todo o histórico; filtre com `--tool` ou `--surface`; equivalente MCP: `pose_usage`. É evidência de uso do produto, não outcome DORA nem score individual de produtividade.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingerem eventos de entrega schema v2 sem identidade individual e calculam métricas para um ambiente de produção explícito (`--environment`, padrão `production`). Deployments exigem `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidentes exigem ambiente; recovery inclui apenas incidentes resolvidos com `--caused-by-deployment`. `adoption-metrics` agrega taxas de especificações, roadmaps e validação de ciclo de vida.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — inspeção de catálogo read-only e offline (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Casa entradas de diretório contra o catálogo mantido de perfis (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — e **.NET**), reportando por perfil: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (múltiplos managers presentes resolvem por prioridade declarada), `confidence` (`medium` sob conflito) e se a ferramenta nativa pré-requisito está no `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, nunca executando um arquivo do projeto. Markers detectados alimentam `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` do mesmo jeito que Node/Go/Rust/Java já fazem; os checks propriamente ditos rodam pelas stacks `python`/`dotnet` da [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json). A prioridade de manager Python é expressa com `when.fileNotExistsAny` (pular quando existir qualquer lockfile/marker de prioridade maior) ao lado dos predicados `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` já existentes.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities. `discover`, `integrate` e `tech-debt` aceitam `--no-persist` para imprimir o resultado sem gravar `.pose/assessments/` nem `.pose/state/`; no checkout selado de uma review delegada (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) eles nunca gravam, `init`, `snapshot` e `request` recusam, e o plano de review os roda com `--no-persist`, para que um revisor que roda um assessment planejado não derrube o próprio run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — diagnósticos de ambiente, dependências, runtime nativo e saúde da instância POSE, além de governança que só falharia mais adiante: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (um profile selecionado exige uma classe que nenhum check pode emitir), `validate.class-producers` (um critério aceita apenas classes que nenhum check registrado produz), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks sem `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set` e `<policy>.policy-keys` para nove políticas (chaves que o motor não lê, ou seja, uma configuração com erro de digitação que silenciosamente não faz nada).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — manutenção idempotente (listar/arquivar/expurgar). Mutações exigem `--apply`. O housekeeping de reports **nunca toca em `history/`**: o JSONL é a fonte de verdade para `recurrence-check` e comparações temporais de `report`. Defaults: stale = 120d, purga de arquivo = 365d.\n - `amend` — histórico append-only de emendas da spec (`amendments.jsonl` ao lado do `spec.md` de uma spec em pasta, ou `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` ao lado de uma spec flat datada; a spec é resolvida pelo store, então os dois layouts recebem emendas do mesmo modo). `--baseline` fotografa o hash de cada R-ID; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` reconhece uma mudança material; `--list` renderiza o histórico e os reconhecimentos pendentes. Em specs `done` com histórico, o `lint-spec` rejeita todo requisito cujo texto atual não esteja reconhecido por um evento — specs não podem ser reescritas em silêncio depois da evidência. `--nodes [--json]` mostra a projeção versionada de contract nodes: requisitos, premissas e decisões com namespace, hash de conteúdo, estado e relações. Com `contract_nodes_version: 1` adotado na policy de review, `--baseline` grava um snapshot R/A/D schema 2, `--ids` aceita A/D, `--change transition` reconhece uma mudança de estado permitida, `editorial` é recusado quando o nó mudou estado ou relações, e o gate também rejeita decisão ativa apoiada em premissa invalidada ou retirada. Sem a capability o gate segue só com requisitos e um evento schema 2 é recusado; logs schema 1 mantêm o significado.\n - `start` — início atômico de uma spec draft, registrado num start record por spec. Sem flags é um preview somente leitura vinculado a digest: readiness e dependências, as obrigações de review declaradas (sem exigir structural delta futuro) e a baseline R/A/D. `--apply --digest <sha256>` grava essa baseline e move a spec de `draft` para `in-progress` com lock por spec e compare-and-swap; exige `atomic_start_version: 1` na policy de review, repete de forma idempotente, retoma uma execução interrompida e recusa outro plano para spec já iniciada. `--cancel` desfaz um start que não moveu a spec. A trava por spec registra seu dono (host, digests dos IDs de máquina e de boot, namespace de PID, PID e início do processo); uma trava de outra chamada é informada, nunca quebrada pela idade, como `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` ou `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Morte só é provada na mesma máquina Linux — outro boot, ou um PID que não roda ou agora é de outro processo — e `--recover-lock <digest do dono>`, com o digest que `--status` mostra, remove essa trava só se os bytes e o dono morto não mudaram, deixando o start record para retomar; um dono desconhecido (outro host, a trava vazia de um motor anterior, outra plataforma) é verificado e removido por uma pessoa. A mesma trava é a trava de ciclo de vida da spec: close e transfer também a tomam, então dois deles nunca se intercalam numa spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifica cada nó como `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` ou `legacy-unbaselined`, recalcula as obrigações e, com a capability, pede reconciliação quando status ou baseline registrada foram editados à mão. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limita a capability às specs criadas a partir dessa data: uma spec mais antiga em andamento sem start aparece como `legacy-unbaselined` com uma nota e não bloqueia nada; sem a data, toda spec assim exige reconciliação. Um start registra precedência observável, não quando algo foi decidido; o MCP expõe a visão somente leitura como `pose_start_status`.\n - `knowledge-usage` — projeta as citações `knowledge:<slug>` das specs por artefato (dono, expiração, specs citantes). Sinais de uso informam a review do dono; o TTL nunca é estendido automaticamente. Refs `knowledge:` órfãs falham no `knowledge-check`.\n - `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — ranqueamento léxico determinístico e projeções consultivas para conhecimento, agrupamento de feedback, marcos de portfólio multi-roadmap e reconciliação de evidências de validação. Sugestões nunca bloqueiam nem se autoaplicam sem confirmação.\n - `hooks` — gerencia symlinks do binário nativo em `.git/hooks/`. O nome de invocação seleciona `check --tolerant` para `pre-commit` e `index` para `post-merge`; `install --force` preserva backup de hooks preexistentes. Uma instância que roda o POSE pelo próprio CLI vinculado a uma revisão declara um executor num arquivo `hooks.json` de `.pose/policy/` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"runner\":\"<executável relativo ao projeto>\"}`): o hook passa a rodar o executor com o nome do hook e devolve o código de saída dele, e `hooks status` o nomeia; um executor fora do projeto, não executável ou com outro schema é recusado e nada roda.\n - `version` — exibe versão compilada do binário Go, SHA do commit e compara a versão do schema do repositório com os requisitos do motor.\n - `context` — resolve um projeto lógico selecionado e uma tarefa opcional, local ou qualificada por `xref:`, pelo resolvedor compartilhado de artefatos. O resultado JSON traz a autoridade canônica, as revisões do projeto e do artefato, o estado de resolução, os contratos suportados e um `context_revision`; nunca inclui roots do filesystem. Use esse token para detectar checkout ou revisão alterados antes de uma escrita entre projetos.\n - Escritas CLI entre projetos exigem o destino em `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` e o `context_revision` de uma chamada recente de `pose context`. Use `new-spec-qualified` para criação qualificada, de modo que motores antigos recusem o próprio comando. Um projeto encontrado apenas por varredura de `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` pode ser lido, mas não concede acesso de escrita. Specs canônicas existentes são reutilizadas; redirecionamentos e transferências incompletas bloqueiam a criação de uma cópia.\n - `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — instala o runtime, regras, workflows, templates e documentação embutidos do POSE em um diretório de destino sem clonar, grava no `adopted_at` da política de changelog o dia em que a instância a recebeu e roda o gate estrito.\n - `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — importa especificações externas para o formato canônico do POSE com frontmatter e 7 seções padronizadas.\n - `serve-mcp` — inicia o servidor POSE Model Context Protocol via stdio (`--stdio`) ou HTTP para integração com agentes de IA. O servidor stdio encerra com SIGTERM; ele resolve o CLI `pose` que executa uma única vez, ao iniciar, então um servidor iniciado antes de um `pose update` continua funcionando com o binário atualizado.\n - `report-limitation` — registra limitação, defeito ou sugestão de melhoria do motor em `.pose/feedback/` com opção de submissão (`--submit`).\n - `contribute <enable|disable|status|stage|list>` — modo contribuidor open-source; registra relatórios sanitizados sob `.pose/contributions/` sem vazar código privado; a submissão permanece a critério do desenvolvedor.\n - `release` / `release-notes` — ciclo de vida governado de releases (`plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`, `status`, `open-next`, `backfill`) garantindo notas de release imutáveis, congelamento de manifesto, importação de evidências de provedor e verificação reproduzível. Uma release que introduz um contrato de governança traz uma seção **Compatibility** no topo das notas, gerada a partir do registro de contratos. `release-notes` fornece alias de compatibilidade para visualização de notas congeladas. A versão com que o corte é comparado vem de `version_source` em `.pose/policy/release.json` (`{\"path\": \"VERSION\", \"kind\": \"text\"}`, ou `\"kind\": \"json\"` com `key`): um arquivo do projeto com `X.Y.Z`, com `v` e sufixo `-dev` opcionais. Só o repositório do motor pode omiti-lo; todo outro projeto precisa declarar um. Integridade de manifest e tag (spec `pose-release-manifest-tag-integrity`): o manifest é lido por um contrato estrito (`schema_version` suportado, sem campos desconhecidos, versão e paths limpos) que recompõe `specs`, `categories`, `breaking` e `release_input_digest` a partir dos fragments que congela, de modo que uma projeção adulterada é recusada em vez de normalizada. `check` e `status` verificam uma tag pela sua própria árvore: a tag (leve ou anotada) é resolvida até o commit, e manifest, notas, fragments congelados e fonte de versão são lidos como blobs desse commit, nunca do worktree. Uma tag cuja árvore não tem manifest é recusada qualquer que seja o worktree; um commit posterior, evidência do provider ou o próximo ciclo não tornam obsoleto um cut correto; uma fonte de versão que não pode ser recomputada a partir do manifest é reportada como limite.\n \n   **Cursores de consulta:** as tools MCP de listagem paginadas (`pose_list_specs`, `pose_list_roadmaps`, `pose_list_knowledge`, `pose_list_reports`, `pose_capability_history`) continuam uma consulta sobre a coleção para a qual o cursor foi emitido. O cursor é assinado, expira em 15 minutos e está ligado à tool, ao projeto, aos filtros e à coleção ordenada com a geração do índice; coleção alterada, outra consulta, token expirado, token versão 1 ou reinício do servidor são recusados com um código `cursor-*` e quem chama recomeça da primeira página, sem nunca continuar sobre uma coleção alterada. Uma página tem no máximo 500 itens, divulga `generation`, `collection` e `total`, e a ausência de `next_cursor` não certifica nada além dessa coleção (spec `pose-generation-bound-query-cursors`).\n \n ### Contrato de conexão MCP\n@@ -1051,29 +1051,31 @@ Declare executor e baseline de implementação antes do dispatch. Um bundle que\n \n ```json\n {\n   \"schema_version\": 1,\n   \"provenance\": {\n     \"trust\": \"executor-attested\",\n     \"implementation\": {\"principal\": \"agent:implementer\", \"execution\": \"implementation-run-1\", \"vendor\": \"vendor-a\", \"model\": \"model-a\"}\n   },\n   \"adapters\": {\n     \"reviewer\": {\"executor\": \"agent:reviewer\", \"command\": [\"reviewer\", \"--output\", \"{output}\"], \"vendor\": \"vendor-b\", \"model\": \"model-b\"}\n   }\n }\n ```\n \n Reutilize o issuer nativo e seu pin de atestações. Configure `authority_project` e `authority_audience` protegidos na policy de review; a identidade declarada do projeto deve concordar. Mantenha chaves fora do projeto. O dispatch captura origem/worktree, bundle/commit/scope/kind, configuração/argv/executável, identidades, template/body/notas do brief, stdin planejado e bytes escritos no pipe, digests de saída/transcript brutos e retidos com redação, além de completude. Escrever no pipe não prova que o fornecedor consumiu o prompt. Sessões e chaves privadas ficam fora da prova pública.\n \n Visualize `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <nome> [--ttl <duração>]` e acrescente `--apply` para publicar uma prova imutável em `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL é opcional (sem expiração quando ausente), positivo quando informado e limitado a 365 dias. Verifique com `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. A prova assina a declaração capturada do executor; não é recibo independente do fornecedor nem confirmação humana. Auditorias failed/interrupted mostram seu estado e `complete_review=false`. Decisão desconhecida ou ambígua também mantém a flag falsa. A flag verdadeira descreve entrega concluída com uma decisão de draft, não aprovação nem validação completa da conclusão tipada. Run completed com stdin incompleto ou saída truncada não estabelece review concluído. Runs legados sem proveniência capturada não são promovidos assinando a configuração atual. `pose review run-proof` e as buscas do emissor dos leitores de veredito fazem a pergunta que o fechamento faz: para uma run cujo escopo está done, a prova como foi dada (o registro se sustenta, o emissor mantém o pin; a deriva rotineira do repositório e a expiração da prova não são perguntadas, porque a expiração limita quando uma prova pode amparar uma nova atestação, não se uma atestação já dada a um escopo done se mantém); para um escopo aberto, como o repositório está agora.\n \n Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicitação completa de `pose review attest`. O issuer deriva do run os vínculos de principal/execução e assina a referência exata. Critérios, tools, evidências e preflight continuam obrigatórios. Draft negativo ou ambíguo não apoia aprovação; a atestação não pode anteceder o término do run ou sua prova assinada. Provas de adjudication/smoke vinculam seus kinds e não servem como atestações de review. Validar/aplicar automaticamente conclusões completas e tipadas pertence à capability, não à assinatura do run.\n \n-**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/8` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/8` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n+**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n \n-**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+\n+**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** Depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria, espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. Um envio que terminou entre reservar a tentativa e registrar o run deixa a tentativa parada e todo envio seguinte do bundle recusado; `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo.\n \n **Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\n \n Ative a barreira de verificação com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review somente ao preparar adoção. Essa barreira viva exige prova de run para review de agente different-actor e recusa prefixo isolado; ela não adiciona nem habilita a capability futura no catálogo. Instâncias não adotantes mantêm o comportamento declared. Com confiança executor-attested explícita, vendor OU modelo normalizado diferente satisfaz different-actor (vendor distinto preferido); baseline desconhecida e mesmo vendor/modelo recusam. Run de máquina nunca satisfaz mandatory-human. Adoção no catálogo, cutoffs/rollback e aplicação automática de conclusões continuam no escopo separado da capability.\n \n Leia a mesma assurance atual por verify, review-check, MCP e `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` identifica a declaração assinada, sua fonte, run/proof e comparação de vendor/modelo. Pins atuais, projeto/audience/tempo, inputs selados, artefatos retidos e receipts originais/de continuação são revalidados. Revogação, receipt ausente, dados reescritos ou inputs stale retiram a assurance de máquina. Assinar não restaura quota nem apaga runs negativos anteriores. O worktree de origem fica registrado com o run, não é exigido de quem lê: uma prova verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto, então um run feito por um job de CI ou pelo Conductor é verificado como um local; não há alegação de contenção universal.\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..69fcb01a\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package cli\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"os/exec\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strconv\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"syscall\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n+)\n+\n+// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1, R2, R4: a\n+// reviewer that ends while a command it started in a session of its own is\n+// still running leaves a run that completes, with that command ended before\n+// the cleanup and named in the run record.\n+func TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning(t *testing.T) {\n+\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"setsid\"); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Skip(\"setsid is not available\")\n+\t}\n+\troot := dispatchInstance(t)\n+\tpidFile := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"leftover.pid\")\n+\tscript := `cat >/dev/null; setsid sh -c 'echo $$ > \"` + pidFile + `\"; while :; do touch \"$TMPDIR/leftover\" 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.05; done' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & ` +\n+\t\t`i=0; while [ ! -s \"` + pidFile + `\" ] && [ $i -lt 100 ]; do sleep 0.05; i=$((i+1)); done; printf 'decision: approved\\n'`\n+\twriteReviewers(t, root, map[string]pose.ReviewerAdapter{\"fake\": {Command: []string{\"sh\", \"-c\", script}, Vendor: \"fixture\", Model: \"fixture\", Containment: &pose.ReviewerContainment{SchemaVersion: 1, Profile: \"trusted-process\"}}})\n+\tcode, out := runPose(t, root, \"delegate\", \"review\", \"spec:alpha\", \"--agent\", \"fake\")\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(pidFile)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the leftover never started: %v (%d %s)\", err, code, out)\n+\t}\n+\tpid, _ := strconv.Atoi(strings.TrimSpace(string(raw)))\n+\tt.Cleanup(func() { _ = syscall.Kill(pid, syscall.SIGKILL) })\n+\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(2 * time.Second)\n+\tfor syscall.Kill(pid, 0) == nil && time.Now().Before(deadline) {\n+\t\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n+\t\t\tbreak\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n+\t}\n+\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && !strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the process the reviewer left running survived the run: %s\", stat)\n+\t}\n+\truns, _, _ := (pose.Store{Root: root}).ReadReviewRuns(\"\")\n+\tif len(runs) != 1 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"runs: %d (%d %s)\", len(runs), code, out)\n+\t}\n+\trun := runs[0]\n+\tif run.Status != pose.ReviewRunCompleted || run.Safety == nil || run.Safety.Operation.Outcome != pose.OutcomeCompleted {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run did not complete with its cleanup: status=%s failure=%s safety=%+v\", run.Status, run.Failure, run.Safety)\n+\t}\n+\tif !strings.Contains(strings.Join(run.Safety.Limitations, \"\\n\"), \"process(es) the run left running\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run record does not name the ended process: %v\", run.Safety.Limitations)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..32c0bc7d\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,101 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"encoding/json\"\n+\t\"errors\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"os/exec\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+)\n+\n+// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R5, R6: a dispatch\n+// that ended between its reservation and its run record leaves an attempt\n+// that blocks the bundle; once its owner is proven gone, cancel releases it\n+// with a failed run and its receipt, and the ledger reads again.\n+func TestAnAbandonedAttemptIsReleasedWithARecord(t *testing.T) {\n+\troot := t.TempDir()\n+\ts := Store{Root: root}\n+\tbundle := \"rvb-00000000000000a1\"\n+\ta := DelegatedReviewAttempt{SchemaVersion: 1, BundleID: bundle, BundleDigest: \"sha256:\" + strings.Repeat(\"a\", 64), Scope: \"spec:alpha\", Adapter: \"fake\", Ordinal: 1,\n+\t\tReservedAt: time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano), Nonce: strings.Repeat(\"0\", 32)}\n+\ta.AttemptID = attemptIdentity(a)\n+\trel, _ := filepath.Rel(root, reviewAttemptPath(root, a))\n+\tif err := publishLedgerJSON(root, filepath.ToSlash(rel), a); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\t// The ledger refuses the bundle while the reservation has no receipt.\n+\tif _, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(a); err == nil || !strings.Contains(err.Error(), \"incomplete or unreadable\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unfinished reservation read as finished: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+\n+\t// The marker the interrupted dispatch left: its owner is a process that\n+\t// has exited.\n+\tdead := exec.Command(\"true\")\n+\tif err := dead.Run(); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + a.AttemptID, Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n+\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n+\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n+\towner.PID = dead.Process.Pid\n+\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: a.AttemptID, RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"b\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: a.Scope, BundleID: bundle,\n+\t\tStartedAt: a.ReservedAt, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: a.ReservedAt, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n+\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n+\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, a.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\n+\trun, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(a.AttemptID, time.Now())\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tif run.Status != ReviewRunFailed || !strings.Contains(run.Failure, \"abandoned\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the release recorded %s: %s\", run.Status, run.Failure)\n+\t}\n+\tif _, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(a); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the released attempt still blocks the bundle: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+\tif left, _ := filepath.Glob(filepath.Join(dir, a.AttemptID+\"*\")); len(left) != 0 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the marker or its stall claim stayed: %v\", left)\n+\t}\n+\t// Released once: a second release finds nothing abandoned.\n+\tif _, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(a.AttemptID, time.Now()); !errors.Is(err, ErrAttemptNotAbandoned) {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a released attempt was released again: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// An attempt whose owner is alive is never released.\n+func TestARunningAttemptIsNotReleased(t *testing.T) {\n+\troot := t.TempDir()\n+\ts := Store{Root: root}\n+\tbundle := \"rvb-00000000000000a2\"\n+\t// Silent for an hour, so only the owner's liveness keeps it running.\n+\told := time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)\n+\ta := DelegatedReviewAttempt{SchemaVersion: 1, BundleID: bundle, BundleDigest: \"sha256:\" + strings.Repeat(\"a\", 64), Scope: \"spec:alpha\", Adapter: \"fake\", Ordinal: 1, ReservedAt: old, Nonce: strings.Repeat(\"0\", 32)}\n+\ta.AttemptID = attemptIdentity(a)\n+\trel, _ := filepath.Rel(root, reviewAttemptPath(root, a))\n+\tif err := publishLedgerJSON(root, filepath.ToSlash(rel), a); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + a.AttemptID, Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n+\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n+\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n+\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: a.AttemptID, RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"d\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: \"spec:alpha\", BundleID: bundle, StartedAt: old, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: old, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n+\tdir, _ := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n+\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n+\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tif _, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(marker.AttemptID, time.Now()); !errors.Is(err, ErrAttemptNotAbandoned) {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a running attempt was released: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\nindex a4f69b9b..1a037471 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n@@ -10,41 +10,41 @@ package pose\n // boundaries; implementer notes are allowed only in a section labelled as\n // the implementer's.\n \n import (\n \t\"crypto/sha256\"\n \t\"encoding/hex\"\n \t\"fmt\"\n \t\"regexp\"\n \t\"sort\"\n \t\"strings\"\n )\n \n // Review brief kinds and their template versions.\n const (\n \tReviewBriefKindReview       = \"review\"\n \tReviewBriefKindAdjudication = \"adjudication\"\n \tReviewBriefKindSmoke        = \"smoke\"\n )\n \n var reviewBriefTemplates = map[string]string{\n-\tReviewBriefKindReview:       reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck,\n+\tReviewBriefKindReview:       reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands,\n \tReviewBriefKindAdjudication: adjudicationBriefTemplateTyped,\n \tReviewBriefKindSmoke:        smokeBriefTemplateTyped,\n }\n \n // The adjudication and smoke templates that ask for a typed verdict (spec\n // pose-delegated-verdict-destinations), and the ones before them.\n const (\n \tadjudicationBriefTemplateTyped = \"adjudication-brief/2\"\n \tadjudicationBriefTemplateFirst = \"adjudication-brief/1\"\n \tsmokeBriefTemplateTyped        = \"smoke-brief/2\"\n \tsmokeBriefTemplateFirst        = \"smoke-brief/1\"\n )\n \n // ReviewBrief is a rendered brief and its identity.\n type ReviewBrief struct {\n \tKind            string `json:\"kind\"`\n \tTemplateVersion string `json:\"template_version\"`\n \tScope           string `json:\"scope\"`\n \tBundleID        string `json:\"bundle_id\"`\n \tBundleDigest    string `json:\"bundle_digest\"`\n@@ -52,56 +52,76 @@ type ReviewBrief struct {\n \tText            string `json:\"text\"`\n }\n \n // The review template that briefs rounds and asks for a pose-draft block\n // (spec pose-delegated-review-round-protocol), and the one before it, kept so\n // a run briefed with it still verifies.\n const (\n \t// A change to the text of a published version takes a new one (spec\n \t// pose-review-brief-templates-are-immutable). /5 stated how a round after the first\n \t// names the findings it accounts for.\n \treviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs = \"review-brief/5\"\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules stated the accepted-risk severities and more, see briefRulesText.\n \treviewBriefTemplateRiskRules = \"review-brief/6\"\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable is the current template: it also says that a draft\n \t// cannot dispense with every required criterion as not-applicable.\n \treviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable = \"review-brief/7\"\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck is the current template: /7 plus the step\n \t// that checks the draft with `pose review draft-check` before the run ends\n \t// (spec pose-review-readiness-before-seal R4).\n \treviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck = \"review-brief/8\"\n+\t// reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools was the current template: /8 plus the\n+\t// instruction to run the planned `pose` commands with the engine that\n+\t// dispatched the run, since the reviewer's shell may resolve an older\n+\t// installed `pose` that ignores flags such as --no-persist (spec\n+\t// pose-assess-leaves-sealed-checkouts-alone R5).\n+\treviewBriefTemplateEngineTools = \"review-brief/9\"\n+\t// reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands is the current template: /9 plus the\n+\t// boundary that every command the reviewer starts ends before it does\n+\t// (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R3).\n+\treviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands = \"review-brief/10\"\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateConclusions asked for the typed conclusion and stated its rules,\n \t// without the identifiers of the findings of earlier rounds.\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateConclusions (/4) asked for the typed conclusion and stated the rules\n \t// the engine checks before it records one (spec pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules).\n \treviewBriefTemplateConclusions = \"review-brief/4\"\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion asked for the conclusion without stating\n \t// those rules; kept so a run briefed with it still verifies and concludes.\n \treviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion = \"review-brief/3\"\n \treviewBriefTemplateRounds          = \"review-brief/2\"\n \treviewBriefTemplateFirst           = \"review-brief/1\"\n )\n \n // reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable reports /7 and the templates that keep its\n // text and add to it.\n func reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template string) bool {\n-\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable || template == reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck\n+\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable || reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template)\n+}\n+\n+// reviewBriefFromDraftCheck reports /8 and the templates that keep its text.\n+func reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template string) bool {\n+\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck || reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template)\n+}\n+\n+// reviewBriefFromEngineTools reports /9 and the templates that keep its text.\n+func reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template string) bool {\n+\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools || template == reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands\n }\n \n // reviewBriefAsksConclusion reports a template that asks for the typed conclusion.\n func reviewBriefAsksConclusion(template string) bool {\n \treturn reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) || template == reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules || template == reviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs || template == reviewBriefTemplateConclusions || template == reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion\n }\n \n // briefRulesTextV5 is the statement of the rules the conclusion is checked against that\n // review-brief/4 and /5 carry, byte for byte; a version never changes its text.\n const briefRulesTextV5 = \"POSE checks the conclusion before it records it, and refuses the whole draft when one rule breaks: (a) a `passed` criterion of kind judgment carries a `rationale` saying what you examined and what you concluded; (b) the `evidence` of a `passed` criterion is exactly one of the refs listed under Sealed evidence (`<class>:<id>`, of a class the criterion asks for when it names one), never a file and line, a command or a structural fact id: those go in a finding or in the `rationale`; (c) a criterion disposed `finding` carries as its `evidence` the `id` of a finding listed in `findings`, and that finding is open until you give it `action`, `owner` and `review_by`; (d) each structural fact listed above is answered by the criterion that asks for structural mapping, as its `mappings`: `[{\\\"delta\\\": <fact id>, \\\"basis\\\": <requirement or decision ref>, \\\"disposition\\\": \\\"mapped\\\", \\\"rationale\\\": <why>}]`, or `not-applicable` with the reason; (e) a `not-applicable` carries a `rationale` and is refused when the bundle seals evidence of a class the criterion asks for; (f) every required criterion and every tool above is answered; (g) the `disposition` of a tool is `passed`, `failed`, `not-used` or `deferred`, never anything else (`not-run` is refused); a tool listed as required must be run and `passed`, with the ref of the sealed evidence it produced as `evidence` when it names classes, and `not-used` is accepted for a required tool only when the plan says no producer exists; a recommended tool you did not run is `not-used` with a `rationale`; (h) the `evidence` of a tool is `<class>:<ref>`: when the tool lists evidence classes, a ref of one of them that the bundle sealed (listed under Sealed evidence), otherwise `command:` followed by the command you ran; a bare command line is refused; (i) a draft that approves leaves no finding open: each finding is either a follow-up with `action`, `owner` and `review_by`, which is recorded as an accepted risk, or the draft requests changes; a finding without them is open and the approval is refused.\"\n \n // briefRulesText is the statement of those rules in the brief of template. review-brief/6 also\n // says which severities the sealed policy lets a follow-up be accepted at, that a tool whose\n // precondition is the completion of the review is answered deferred, the component scoping of\n // evidence, and what a mapping's basis is.\n func briefRulesText(template string, bundle ReviewBundle) string {\n \tif template != reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules && !reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) {\n \t\treturn briefRulesTextV5\n \t}\n \ttext := briefRulesTextV5\n@@ -110,41 +130,41 @@ func briefRulesText(template string, bundle ReviewBundle) string {\n \t\tseverities = \"none\"\n \t}\n \ttext = strings.Replace(text, \"a recommended tool you did not run is `not-used` with a `rationale`;\", \"a recommended tool you did not run is `not-used` with a `rationale`; a required tool whose precondition is the completion of the review (`review-check`, `closeout-check`) cannot pass before your attestation: answer it `deferred` with a `rationale`;\", 1)\n \ttext = strings.Replace(text, \"which is recorded as an accepted risk, or the draft requests changes;\", \"which is recorded as an accepted risk only when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (\"+severities+\"), or the draft requests changes and any finding of another severity is fixed first;\", 1)\n \tif reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) {\n \t\ttext += \" (l) a draft that dispenses with every required criterion as `not-applicable` is refused: a scope whose whole plan is inapplicable is not reviewed by it;\"\n \t}\n \treturn text + \" (j) the `evidence` of a `passed` criterion is of a class the criterion asks for and, when the criterion or tool belongs to a component, of that component (the Sealed evidence list names the module of each ref); (k) the `basis` of a mapping is a requirement or decision id of the scope's spec, such as R1 or D1.\"\n }\n \n // reviewBriefStatesRules reports a template that states the rules the conclusion is\n // checked against.\n func reviewBriefStatesRules(template string) bool {\n \treturn reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) || template == reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules || template == reviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs || template == reviewBriefTemplateConclusions\n }\n \n // reviewBriefEarlierTemplates are the review templates a run may have been\n // briefed with before the current one, newest first; a run briefed with one\n // is re-rendered with it to verify and to continue (the brief a run was sent\n // never changes under it).\n-var reviewBriefEarlierTemplates = []string{reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable, reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules, reviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs, reviewBriefTemplateConclusions, reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion, reviewBriefTemplateRounds, reviewBriefTemplateFirst}\n+var reviewBriefEarlierTemplates = []string{reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools, reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck, reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable, reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules, reviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs, reviewBriefTemplateConclusions, reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion, reviewBriefTemplateRounds, reviewBriefTemplateFirst}\n \n // briefEarlierTemplates are, per kind, the templates a run may have been\n // briefed with before the current one, newest first.\n var briefEarlierTemplates = map[string][]string{\n \tReviewBriefKindReview:       reviewBriefEarlierTemplates,\n \tReviewBriefKindAdjudication: {adjudicationBriefTemplateFirst},\n \tReviewBriefKindSmoke:        {smokeBriefTemplateFirst},\n }\n \n // ReviewBriefKinds lists the accepted kinds.\n func ReviewBriefKinds() []string {\n \treturn []string{ReviewBriefKindReview, ReviewBriefKindAdjudication, ReviewBriefKindSmoke}\n }\n \n // RenderReviewBrief renders the brief of kind for target, a bundle id or a\n // scope ref, from its current sealed bundle. A superseded bundle is refused:\n // a brief for it would ask a reviewer to judge content that has moved.\n func (s Store) RenderReviewBrief(target, kind string, notes string) (ReviewBrief, error) {\n \treturn s.renderReviewBriefAs(target, kind, notes, \"\")\n }\n@@ -302,40 +322,43 @@ func renderReviewBriefBody(bundle ReviewBundle, kind, template, notes string, sm\n \n \tline(\"## Task\")\n \tline(\"\")\n \tswitch kind {\n \tcase ReviewBriefKindReview:\n \t\tline(\"Review the change below against the scope's requirements and the criteria the plan owes. Read the code and run the tests you need; judge each criterion with your own evidence.\")\n \tcase ReviewBriefKindAdjudication:\n \t\tline(\"Give an independent verdict on the questions the scope records (its decisions, outcomes and requirement trace), using only the sealed evidence and what you can reproduce. Say for each whether the record holds, and why.\")\n \tcase ReviewBriefKindSmoke:\n \t\tline(\"Exercise each delivered surface of the scope from its entrypoint, as a user of it would, and report what you observe against what the spec says should happen. Do not review the code beyond what you need to run it.\")\n \t}\n \tline(\"\")\n \n \tline(\"## Boundaries\")\n \tline(\"\")\n \tline(\"- Report every defect with file, line and severity (high, medium, low). Do not fix it.\")\n \tline(\"- Do not change the repository: no edits, commits, pushes, tags or releases.\")\n \tline(\"- Do not record an attestation: POSE records the conclusion of a verified run.\")\n \tline(\"- Do not reveal secrets or key material you come across.\")\n \tline(\"- If something you need is missing, say what and stop, rather than assuming it.\")\n+\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands {\n+\t\tline(\"- Let every command you start finish before you end, and do not leave anything running in the background: POSE ends what is left and records it, and a long run left behind costs time you could have used.\")\n+\t}\n \tline(\"\")\n \n \tline(\"## Change\")\n \tline(\"\")\n \tline(\"- range: %s..%s\", orNone(subject.Base), orNone(subject.Head))\n \tline(\"- change sets: %s\", orNone(strings.Join(subject.ChangeSets, \", \")))\n \tentries := append([]ReviewBundleSubjectEntry(nil), subject.Entries...)\n \tsort.Slice(entries, func(i, j int) bool { return entryPath(entries[i]) < entryPath(entries[j]) })\n \tfor _, e := range entries {\n \t\tline(\"- %s %s (%s)\", e.Action, entryPath(e), e.Class)\n \t}\n \tline(\"\")\n \n \tif smoke != nil {\n \t\tline(\"## Surfaces to run\")\n \t\tline(\"\")\n \t\tif len(smoke.Surfaces) == 0 {\n \t\t\tline(\"The scope declares no delivery targets. Report that there is nothing to smoke and stop.\")\n \t\t}\n \t\tfor _, t := range smoke.Surfaces {\n@@ -358,40 +381,44 @@ func renderReviewBriefBody(bundle ReviewBundle, kind, template, notes string, sm\n \tline(\"## Criteria to answer\")\n \tline(\"\")\n \tcriteria := append([]ReviewPlanCriterion(nil), plan.Criteria...)\n \tsort.Slice(criteria, func(i, j int) bool { return criteria[i].ID < criteria[j].ID })\n \tfor _, c := range criteria {\n \t\trequired := \"optional\"\n \t\tif c.Required {\n \t\t\trequired = \"required\"\n \t\t}\n \t\tevidence := \"\"\n \t\tif len(c.EvidenceClasses) > 0 {\n \t\t\tevidence = \"; evidence: \" + strings.Join(c.EvidenceClasses, \", \")\n \t\t}\n \t\tline(\"- %s (%s, %s%s): %s\", c.ID, ReviewCriterionKind(c), required, evidence, c.Description)\n \t}\n \tline(\"\")\n \n \tif len(plan.Tools) > 0 {\n \t\tline(\"## Tools the plan requires\")\n \t\tline(\"\")\n+\t\tif reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template) {\n+\t\t\tline(\"Run each `pose` command below as `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\"` followed by the same arguments: it is the engine that sealed and dispatched this run. The `pose` your shell resolves may be an older installed engine that ignores flags such as `--no-persist` and writes into this sealed checkout, which fails the run.\")\n+\t\t\tline(\"\")\n+\t\t}\n \t\ttools := append([]ReviewPlanTool(nil), plan.Tools...)\n \t\tsort.SliceStable(tools, func(i, j int) bool { return tools[i].ID+tools[i].Component < tools[j].ID+tools[j].Component })\n \t\tfor _, t := range tools {\n \t\t\tcomponent := \"\"\n \t\t\tif t.Component != \"\" {\n \t\t\t\tcomponent = \" [\" + t.Component + \"]\"\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t\tclasses := \"\"\n \t\t\tif reviewBriefStatesRules(template) && len(t.EvidenceClasses) > 0 {\n \t\t\t\tclasses = \"; evidence classes: \" + strings.Join(t.EvidenceClasses, \", \")\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t\tline(\"- %s%s (%s%s): `%s`\", t.ID, component, t.Requiredness, classes, strings.Join(t.Args, \" \"))\n \t\t}\n \t\tline(\"\")\n \t}\n \n \tif plan.Structure != nil && len(plan.Structure.Material) > 0 {\n \t\tline(\"## Structural facts to answer for\")\n \t\tline(\"\")\n \t\tline(\"Map each fact to the requirement or decision that causes it, or say that nothing does.\")\n@@ -468,41 +495,41 @@ func renderReviewBriefBody(bundle ReviewBundle, kind, template, notes string, sm\n \t\treturn b.String()\n \t}\n \tline(\"1. A decision: `approved` or `changes-requested`.\")\n \tline(\"2. For each criterion above: passed, failed or not-applicable, with your evidence (a sealed evidence ref, a command you ran, or file and line).\")\n \tnext := 3\n \tif plan.Structure != nil && len(plan.Structure.Material) > 0 {\n \t\tline(\"3. For each structural fact: the requirement or decision it maps to.\")\n \t\tline(\"4. Every defect, with file, line and severity.\")\n \t\tnext = 5\n \t} else {\n \t\tline(\"3. Every defect, with file, line and severity.\")\n \t\tnext = 4\n \t}\n \tif round != nil {\n \t\tline(\"%d. At the end, the draft POSE reads: one fenced block opened with ```pose-draft holding a JSON object with `schema` %q, `decision` (the decision above), `findings` (each with `id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line` and `summary`)%s. Everything outside the block is yours and is not read.\", next, ReviewDraftSchema, roundFields(*round, template))\n \t\tif conclusion {\n \t\t\tline(\"%d. In the same block, the conclusion POSE records as the attestation: `criteria` (one entry per criterion above, each with `id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>` — a class the criterion asks for when it names one — and `rationale` for not-applicable), and `tools` (one entry per tool above, each with `id`, `component` when it has one, `disposition`, `evidence` and `rationale`). A follow-up finding that should be accepted as a risk carries `action`, `owner` and `review_by`; any other finding stays open. A criterion or tool you do not answer is not answered for you.\", next+1)\n \t\t\tif reviewBriefStatesRules(template) {\n \t\t\t\tline(\"%d. %s\", next+2, briefRulesText(template, bundle))\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck {\n+\t\t\tif reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template) {\n \t\t\t\tline(\"%d. Before you finish, check your final message: pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`, the engine that dispatched this run (it reads this run's bundle and round, writes nothing and works in a read-only sandbox) and fix every reason it reports. A draft it refuses is refused at conclusion, and the round is spent.\", next+3)\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n \t}\n \tif template == reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules || reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) {\n \t\t// ParseReviewDraft refuses a draft whose `decision:` line outside the block disagrees\n \t\t// with the block, so the brief cannot say the outside is not read at all.\n \t\t// The last occurrence is the instruction of the step; an earlier one may be in the notes.\n \t\tout := b.String()\n \t\tconst sentence = \"Everything outside the block is yours and is not read.\"\n \t\tif at := strings.LastIndex(out, sentence); at >= 0 {\n \t\t\tout = out[:at] + \"Everything outside the block is yours and is not read, except a `decision:` line, which must agree with the decision of the block.\" + out[at+len(sentence):]\n \t\t}\n \t\treturn out\n \t}\n \treturn b.String()\n }\n \n func roundFields(round ReviewRound, template string) string {\n \tif round.Number < 2 {\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\nindex 79ab62fd..41272067 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n@@ -41,51 +41,51 @@ func TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tfor _, phrase := range phrases {\n \t\t\tif !strings.Contains(brief.Text, phrase) {\n \t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"the brief does not state the rule behind %q (%q)\", blocker, phrase)\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n \t}\n }\n \n // R3: a run briefed with review-brief/3 still renders as it did, without the rules, so its\n // digest still verifies; the current template is /6.\n func TestAnEarlierConclusionBriefStillRendersWithoutTheRules(t *testing.T) {\n \tstore, bundle := briefFixture(t)\n \tcurrent, err := store.RenderReviewBrief(bundle.BundleID, \"\", \"\")\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\tt.Fatal(err)\n \t}\n \tearlier, err := store.renderReviewBriefAs(bundle.BundleID, ReviewBriefKindReview, \"\", reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion)\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\tt.Fatal(err)\n \t}\n-\tif current.TemplateVersion != \"review-brief/8\" || earlier.TemplateVersion != \"review-brief/3\" {\n+\tif current.TemplateVersion != \"review-brief/10\" || earlier.TemplateVersion != \"review-brief/3\" {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"templates: %s %s\", current.TemplateVersion, earlier.TemplateVersion)\n \t}\n \tif strings.Contains(earlier.Text, \"POSE checks the conclusion before it records it\") {\n \t\tt.Fatal(\"review-brief/3 changed under the runs briefed with it\")\n \t}\n \tif !strings.Contains(earlier.Text, \"the conclusion POSE records as the attestation\") || earlier.Digest == current.Digest {\n \t\tt.Fatal(\"review-brief/3 no longer asks for the conclusion, or has the digest of /4\")\n \t}\n-\tif !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/3\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/4\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/5\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/6\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/7\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/8\") || reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/2\") {\n-\t\tt.Fatal(\"the templates that ask for a conclusion are /3 to /8\")\n+\tif !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/3\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/4\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/5\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/6\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/7\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/8\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/9\") || !reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/10\") || reviewBriefAsksConclusion(\"review-brief/2\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"the templates that ask for a conclusion are /3 to /10\")\n \t}\n }\n \n // R4: the Claude reviewer profile allows read-only commands in the three commands it is\n // launched with, and nothing that writes.\n func TestTheClaudeReviewerProfileAllowsOnlyReadOnlyCommands(t *testing.T) {\n \traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \".pose\", \"policy\", \"delegation.json\"))\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\tt.Skipf(\"no delegation policy at the repository root: %v\", err)\n \t}\n \tvar policy struct {\n \t\tAgents map[string]struct {\n \t\t\tCommands map[string][]string `json:\"commands\"`\n \t\t\tResume   struct {\n \t\t\t\tCommand  []string            `json:\"command\"`\n \t\t\t\tCommands map[string][]string `json:\"commands\"`\n \t\t\t} `json:\"resume\"`\n \t\t} `json:\"agents\"`\n \t}\n \tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &policy); err != nil {\n@@ -135,20 +135,48 @@ func TestTheBriefOfALaterRoundStatesTheIdentifiersOfPriorFindings(t *testing.T)\n \t}\n \tearlier := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplateConclusions, \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 2}, true, nil)\n \tif strings.Contains(earlier, \"never a bare F1\") {\n \t\tt.Error(\"review-brief/4 changed under its version: the rule belongs to review-brief/5 and later\")\n \t}\n }\n \n // Spec pose-review-readiness-before-seal R4: the current brief asks the\n // reviewer to check its draft before finishing; /7 never did.\n func TestTheCurrentBriefAsksForTheDraftCheck(t *testing.T) {\n \tconst step = \"pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`\"\n \tcurrent := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplates[ReviewBriefKindReview], \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\n \tif !strings.Contains(current, step) {\n \t\tt.Fatal(\"the current review brief does not ask for the draft check\")\n \t}\n \tearlier := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable, \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\n \tif strings.Contains(earlier, step) {\n \t\tt.Fatal(\"review-brief/7 changed under the runs briefed with it\")\n \t}\n }\n+\n+// Spec pose-assess-leaves-sealed-checkouts-alone R5: the current brief has\n+// the planned `pose` commands run by the dispatching engine; /8 never did.\n+func TestTheCurrentBriefRunsPlannedToolsWithTheDispatchingEngine(t *testing.T) {\n+\tconst step = \"Run each `pose` command below as `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\"`\"\n+\tcurrent := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplates[ReviewBriefKindReview], \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\n+\tif !strings.Contains(current, step) || !strings.Contains(current, \"pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"the current review brief does not run planned tools and the draft check with the dispatching engine\")\n+\t}\n+\tearlier := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck, \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\n+\tif strings.Contains(earlier, step) {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"review-brief/8 changed under the runs briefed with it\")\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R3: the current\n+// brief asks the reviewer to let its commands finish; /9 never did.\n+func TestTheCurrentBriefAsksCommandsToFinish(t *testing.T) {\n+\tconst boundary = \"Let every command you start finish before you end\"\n+\tcurrent := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplates[ReviewBriefKindReview], \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\n+\tif !strings.Contains(current, boundary) || !strings.Contains(current, \"Run each `pose` command below as\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"the current review brief does not keep /9 and ask commands to finish\")\n+\t}\n+\tearlier := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools, \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\n+\tif strings.Contains(earlier, boundary) {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"review-brief/9 changed under the runs briefed with it\")\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..7e97d7dd\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+// Leftover reviewer processes (spec\n+// pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1): an adapter such as\n+// `codex exec` runs each command in a session of its own, so killing the\n+// adapter's process group misses a command the reviewer left running, and\n+// that command keeps writing into the run's runtime directory while the\n+// cleanup inventory reads it. Every process of the run inherits\n+// SealedCheckoutEnv naming the run's checkout, so the dispatch finds them by\n+// that entry, ends them and waits for them before the inventory.\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"bytes\"\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strconv\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"syscall\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+)\n+\n+const reviewReapWait = 5 * time.Second\n+\n+// reapSealedCheckoutProcesses ends every process whose environment names\n+// worktree as its sealed checkout and returns a description of each. An\n+// error means a process could not be ended within the bound.\n+func reapSealedCheckoutProcesses(worktree string) ([]string, error) {\n+\tif worktree == \"\" {\n+\t\treturn nil, nil\n+\t}\n+\tentry := []byte(SealedCheckoutEnv + \"=\" + worktree)\n+\tself := os.Getpid()\n+\tfind := func() map[int]string {\n+\t\tfound := map[int]string{}\n+\t\tdirs, err := os.ReadDir(\"/proc\")\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn found\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor _, d := range dirs {\n+\t\t\tpid, err := strconv.Atoi(d.Name())\n+\t\t\tif err != nil || pid == self {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tenviron, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", d.Name(), \"environ\"))\n+\t\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tfor _, kv := range bytes.Split(environ, []byte{0}) {\n+\t\t\t\tif bytes.Equal(kv, entry) {\n+\t\t\t\t\tfound[pid] = reviewProcessName(pid)\n+\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\treturn found\n+\t}\n+\tvar reaped []string\n+\tseen := map[int]bool{}\n+\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(reviewReapWait)\n+\tfor {\n+\t\talive := find()\n+\t\tfor pid, name := range alive {\n+\t\t\tif reviewProcessZombie(pid) {\n+\t\t\t\tdelete(alive, pid)\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif !seen[pid] {\n+\t\t\t\tseen[pid] = true\n+\t\t\t\treaped = append(reaped, fmt.Sprintf(\"%d %s\", pid, name))\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t_ = syscall.Kill(pid, syscall.SIGKILL)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif len(alive) == 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn reaped, nil\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif time.Now().After(deadline) {\n+\t\t\treturn reaped, fmt.Errorf(\"%d process(es) of the run could not be ended\", len(alive))\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessName(pid int) string {\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", strconv.Itoa(pid), \"comm\"))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn \"?\"\n+\t}\n+\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(raw))\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessZombie(pid int) bool {\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", strconv.Itoa(pid), \"stat\"))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn true // gone\n+\t}\n+\t// The state follows the parenthesised command name.\n+\tif i := bytes.LastIndexByte(raw, ')'); i >= 0 && i+2 < len(raw) {\n+\t\treturn raw[i+2] == 'Z'\n+\t}\n+\treturn false\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..a32e5b2b\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+//go:build !linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+// reapSealedCheckoutProcesses has no portable way to read another process's\n+// environment; outside Linux the process-group kill is the only termination.\n+func reapSealedCheckoutProcesses(string) ([]string, error) { return nil, nil }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\nindex f4665a92..25b9133e 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n@@ -403,41 +403,41 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - Assessment freshness (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — a component assessment records its binding: the committed tree of the component, the engine version and the validation-matrix digest. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reuses an assessment whose binding is unchanged and refreshes it otherwise, printing `assess.freshness.<component>` with the reason (content changed, engine changed, matrix changed, uncommitted changes, or no binding on an older record). Age alone never stales an assessment. A missing or stale assessment of a component an in-progress spec declares appears as an advisory `evidence` obligation in `state --attention`; it gates nothing.\n - `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lists open follow-ups with a reconciliation reason: `target-terminal` (a spec the follow-up names is done, superseded or abandoned), `evidence-present` (a test it names now exists), `overdue` (its review date passed), plus lexical near-duplicate clusters. Each candidate shows its evidence and its limits; nothing is dispositioned, similarity never becomes `duplicate` without judgment, and an open follow-up never leaves Attention without a disposition written in its spec. Attention shows the same reason on the residual-debt item.\n - `action list --present [--actor <id|role>] [--json]` (spec `pose-action-request-presentation`) — groups the requests still waiting for an answer for one conversation: by origin and earliest restricted phase, the ones restricting start or execution first (`interrupt`), release-only ones marked as able to wait. Each request keeps its own id, request digest, revision and answer, and shows exactly the content a resolution binds to; satisfied requests are not asked again. MCP: `pose_action_requests` with `present: true`. Deciding when to interrupt a person is left to the consumer (Harne8).\n - Progressive spec surface (spec `pose-progressive-spec-surface`) — `pose new-spec <slug> --surface minimal|standard|full` records `surface:` in the frontmatter. A `minimal` spec keeps Intent, Requirements, the Technical Plan with its Artifacts, Validation with the requirement trace and the Final Report with its follow-ups; it drops the planner-local Tasks section and the report subsections that `pose specs facts <slug> [--json]` derives from the delivery index (paths of the attributed commits, checks that produced scoped evidence). `lint-spec` stops requiring Tasks for a minimal spec and relaxes nothing else: the trace, follow-up and amendment gates apply as before. The facts never include intent, rationale or accepted risk. Evidence binding (spec `pose-spec-facts-evidence-binding`): `specs facts` separates `checks` (results whose scope provenance names the spec, with their basis and whether they ran against the head of the spec's change sets) from `candidates` (results the index holds that nothing ties to the spec: a declared module or an empty `components` is not provenance); candidates are printed as `CANDIDATE, not evidence of this spec`. `freshness` reports `fresh`, `stale` (an input changed since the index was published), `unknown` (never published as a set) or `unavailable`, and a limitation says to run `pose index` when it cannot be established; reading the facts never rebuilds the index and never concludes that a requirement is satisfied.\n - **Automatic project-state refresh** (spec `pose-project-state-refresh-contract`) — an internal post-event hook registry fires a **partial** `pose state refresh` (only the sections an event affects) at the points POSE already intercepts: a successful `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict` on a `status: done` spec (`spec_closeout` → Specs & Roadmaps, Follow-ups, Validation & Evidence), `pose amend` (`spec_amend` → Specs & Roadmaps, Decisions & Knowledge), `pose reconcile-evidence record` (`evidence_reconciled` → Validation & Evidence) and `pose assess snapshot` (`assessment_snapshot` → Capabilities). When the event carries a commit and `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured, the consumer calls `components_hit` (spec `graphforge-components-hit-contract`) from the state's baseline to the event's commit and attaches the hit components to the Architecture section (**directed** refresh); without GraphForge configured, refresh stays full/undirected — zero build coupling. No daemon, no filesystem watcher: it is a synchronous call at the exact point each event already happens. **Best-effort by default**: a failed refresh never blocks the command that fired the event — it marks `refresh_pending: <event>` in the state frontmatter instead (cleared by the next successful refresh, regardless of trigger). Strict mode is opt-in via policy (`strict_refresh: true`) — there, a refresh failure fails the triggering command. Every run (triggered or manual) is logged to the artifact's own append-only log (metadata only: trigger, target, result `ok|failed|skipped`, duration, changed-section hashes — never content). Dedup key `hash(event+target+commit)`: the same event processed twice (retry/replay) does not repeat the refresh, result `skipped`; `manual`/`ci` refreshes are never deduped against each other (an explicit call always runs). `release_cut` is registered in the event→sections map but has no producer inside pose-mcp today — cutting a release belongs to Conductor (a separate service); the trigger is ready for when that integration exists.\n - **Capability reassessment triggers** (spec `pose-capability-assessment-triggers`, same hook registry as the automatic project-state refresh above) — the `assessment-staleness` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components a closeout reached (via `components_hit` when `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured; fallback: intersecting the event's touched files against each mechanism's manually declared `paths:` globs in the assessment) and marks every affected capability mechanism stale — never mutating a score, only recording `since`/`trigger`/`hits` and projecting a cobrável demand (origin `assessment-trigger`) into `pose followups --open`, with no duplicate storage. `pose assess snapshot` clears the marks on the mechanisms it reassesses and records the mark→snapshot link in the history. On-demand: `pose assess stale [--json]` lists pending marks; `pose assess request --mechanism <id> [--reason <text>]` creates one manually (the same path a UI-driven \"flag for reassessment\" action would call over MCP). MCP tool: `pose_capability_stale`. Without GraphForge and without `paths:` declared, the event logs `capability_mapping_unavailable` to the refresh log — a visible signal, nothing marked silently. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level` `direct`/`any`, the demand's default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy file already used for age/commit-based staleness (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\n - **Docs governance** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — an opt-in, per-project contract for documentation, same mechanic as the capability assessment above: absent, every project stays valid everywhere. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` scaffolds a manifest declaring governed doc roots and, per doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, or a custom value)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/optional `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` runs seven deterministic, offline rules — a declared doc missing from disk, a file present but undeclared, missing minimal frontmatter (`title`/`doc_type`), a broken relative link, a broken typed reference (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (an entry's own review date, or the manifest's default review window counted from its last touching commit) and a security scan reusing the same deterministic unsafe-instruction/secret-shaped pattern check skills already run — each with a configurable severity (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorporates `docs-check` when the manifest exists (opt-in by presence); only errors block, warnings surface without blocking. `pose docs-sync` synchronizes governance metadata. Tool MCP: `pose_docs_state`. Project-state gains an additive Docs section (manifest presence/profile/roots, declared/undeclared/stale/error/warning counts).\n - **Docs review-pending triggers** (spec `pose-docs-assessment-followups`, third consumer of the same hook registry as the two entries above — reused unmodified) — a `docs-review` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components/files a closeout reached (`components_hit` when configured, matched against `owns:` entries declared as `component:<id>`; otherwise the event's touched files matched against each doc's `owns:` paths/globs, where a directory like `\"site\"` covers every file under it) and marks every doc whose declared area was reached as review-pending — never editing the doc itself, never touching a score. Marks accumulate in an append-only log outside the doc's own file and project a synthetic, owned demand into `pose followups --open` (origin `docs:<doc-path>`), reusing the manifest entry's own `owner` field when declared. `pose docs-review resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]` closes every mark pending on a doc at once, recording `updated` (default, captures the current commit) or `no_change_needed` (reason required); `pose docs-review request <doc>`/`--all-stale` cover the on-demand path — the latter bridges every currently-stale doc into an active demand in one call. `docs-check`'s own output and `pose_docs_state` additively list what's still pending. Without a component map and without `owns:` declared, the event logs a visible signal instead of marking anything silently — here the path fallback is the mechanism's full-strength path (`owns:` is expressed as paths by default), not a lesser one. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level`, default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy shape as the capability triggers in its own file (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\n - `history-check` — verifies every `.jsonl` under `reports/history/` is git-tracked. Without that, `recurrence-check` and `stats` diverge between machines. Strict blocks; tolerant warns.\n - `suggest` — reads [`task-map.json`](.pose/indexes/task-map.json) and prints the canonical trail (workflow + skill + rules + spec/ADR + knowledge) for a task type. With no arguments it lists every type. `--domain <d>` adds domain rules (frontend, backend-go, k8s); `--path <p>` infers the domain heuristically and via [`repo-map.json`](.pose/indexes/repo-map.json) (`language` → frontend/backend-go); `--json` for agents. Domains (spec `pose-distributed-instructions-parity`): `--domain` and the domain inferred from `--path` go through one taxonomy (`go`/`golang`/`backend-go` → `backend-go`; `react`/`frontend-react`/`frontend` → `frontend`; `kubernetes`/`helm`/`k8s` → `k8s`), the JSON discloses `domain_input` and `domain_source: alias`, and a domain the task map has no rules for is reported (`domain_status`) instead of borrowing another's; a `backend` module is the Go domain only when its language is Go. `rules_status` (and the human output) says for each rule whether it is `installed` (with its path), `extension-needed` (with `pose extension install <id>`) or `unavailable`; suggest announces no rule file that is not on disk and installs nothing.\n - `stats` — aggregates history JSONL outcomes by workflow, task or context. Enables objective decisions (promoting a check from optional to required, spotting unstable workflows, comparing ci against manual). `--since-days N` filters the window; `--json` for machine consumption.\n - `stats governance` — schema 2 read-only projection over report history, sealed review bundles, attestations and explicit remediation declarations. It keeps preparation, judgment, intervention, freshness, telemetry, provenance and coverage independent, never emits a quality score, and reports missing/invalid data explicitly. `--report-type` filters validation attempts; `--band` filters reviews and finding links. Replay identity is `(report_type, task_slug, sequence)`; identity-less rows remain visible in coverage. MCP `pose_governance_stats` accepts the same selectors and requires `project_id` when choosing a federated root. Explicit finding links contain only opaque IDs, spec slugs, category and source band, with at most 100 rows and truncation reported.\n - `stats governance --waits --rework` (spec `pose-governance-wait-rework-observability`) — two more dimensions beside the outcomes, never folded into a score. With `--waits` or `--rework` only the requested dimensions are computed; `--outcomes` adds the outcomes, which read the whole history. `--waits` times action requests and keeps three measures apart: the age of each request, the interval attributed to waiting on an actor or an external system, and `known_blocking_seconds`, the union per spec of intervals in which a request restricted the whole spec's execution (simultaneous requests count once). None of them is inactivity; missing timestamps are counted as unknown. `--rework` classifies superseded sealed bundles by what changed (subject, intent or plan, review plan, evidence, or unknown) and extra attestations on one bundle by review disagreement or an earlier attestation invalid against today's policy, leaving other causes unknown. `scripts/bench-governance.sh [runs] [pose-binary]` times `state --attention`, `check --strict` and `index` on the same revision and reports time and the index size separately, with the corpus revision and engine version.\n - Premise validity (spec `pose-assumption-validity-scope`) — a material `Assumption A<N>` may declare `Valid scope:` (the context it holds in) and `Stale trigger:` entries `<kind>:<value>@<pin>`, where kind is `doc`, `report`, `knowledge`, `adr` or `snapshot` and the pin is a prefix (8+ hex) of the sha256 of the content the premise was judged against. `lint-spec --design-check` reports an unpinned trigger with the pin to append, an unreadable one as unknown, and a calendar trigger (`date:`, `ttl:`, …) as not evaluated: validity is by context, not expiry, and trivial premises need neither field. When pinned content changes, the obligation projection (producer `premises`) emits a `premise-stale` judgment obligation for draft and in-progress specs, advisory on review and scoped to the assumption and the decisions based on it. The recorded evidence stays; it is described as judged against the pinned content and not valid for the current one, and nothing is invalidated automatically. Provider or remote contracts are not monitored by the core. Trigger state is projection only and never changes the design digest.\n - Falsifier observation (spec `pose-falsifier-reconsideration`) — a material `Decision D<N>` may add `Expected effect:` and `Falsifier check: check:<module>/<check>`, naming the indexed validation check whose failure contradicts it. When the delivery index holds a `fail` result for that check, the obligation projection (producer `falsifiers`) emits a `falsifier-observed` judgment obligation, advisory on review and scoped to the decision, for any spec that is not superseded or abandoned (decisions stay in force after done). The engine infers no cause and judges no architecture: the failure is an observation, the decision and its rationale are never edited, and a person either records why it still holds or opens a superseding decision. A passing or absent result raises nothing. `lint-spec --design-check` warns on a malformed reference and on a falsifier check without a stated falsifier.\n - `migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]` (spec `pose-v7-legacy-cleanup-plan`) — read-only inventory of what a future major would convert, keep or refuse: spec layouts, `blocked` specs, legacy and unknown review policy keys, sealed bundles, attestations by identity mode and attribution, and incomplete transfers on this project's side, each with its action and removal criterion. It writes nothing. Sealed bundles, attestations and signatures are never rewritten. `--apply` is refused with the current fact: no apply is available in this engine, the only mode is `--dry-run`, which writes nothing; no future apply is promised.\n - `assess design --spec <slug>` — read-only bounded projection over the canonical sealed review subject. It observes dependency, component, delivery-metadata, governance-contract and public-contract deltas, keeps unknown/unsupported coverage explicit, and never computes a complexity or architecture score. The MCP equivalent is `pose_design_delta`.\n - `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\n - `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\n - `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\n - `knowledge-usage` — projects `knowledge:<slug>` citations from specs per artifact (owner, expiry, citing specs). Usage signals inform the owner's review; TTL is never extended automatically. Dangling `knowledge:` refs fail `knowledge-check`.\n - `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — deterministic lexical ranking and advisory projections for knowledge, feedback clustering, multi-roadmap portfolio milestones, and delivery validation evidence reconciliation. Suggestions never gate or auto-apply without confirmation.\n - `hooks` — links the native binary into `.git/hooks/`; invocation name selects `check --tolerant` for `pre-commit` and `index` for `post-merge`. `install --force` keeps a backup of pre-existing hooks. An instance that runs POSE through its own revision-bound CLI declares a runner in a `hooks.json` file of `.pose/policy/` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"runner\":\"<project-relative executable>\"}`): the hook then runs the runner with the hook's name and returns its exit code, and `hooks status` names it; a runner outside the project, not executable or under another schema is refused and nothing runs.\n - `version` — displays compiled Go binary version, commit SHA, and compares the repository schema version with engine requirements.\n - `context` — resolves a selected logical project and optional local or `xref:`-qualified task through the shared artifact resolver. The JSON result includes canonical authority, project/artifact revisions, resolution state, supported contracts and a `context_revision`; it never includes filesystem roots. Use this token to detect a changed checkout or revision before a cross-project write.\n - Cross-project CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the `context_revision` from a fresh `pose context` call. Use `new-spec-qualified` for qualified creation so older engines reject the command itself. A project found only by `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` scan can be read but does not grant write access. Existing canonical specs are reused; redirects and incomplete transfers block shadow creation.\n - `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — installs the embedded POSE runtime, rules, workflows, templates, and documentation into a target project directory without cloning, stamps `adopted_at` in the changelog policy with the day the instance received it, and runs the strict gate.\n - `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — imports foreign specification trees into canonical POSE specifications with standard frontmatter and section structure.\n - `serve-mcp` — starts the POSE Model Context Protocol server over stdio (`--stdio`) or HTTP for native AI agent integration. The stdio server exits on SIGTERM; it resolves the `pose` CLI it runs once, at start, so a server started before `pose update` keeps working with the updated binary.\n - `report-limitation` — records an engine limitation, defect, or enhancement proposal into `.pose/feedback/` with optional submission (`--submit`).\n - `contribute <enable|disable|status|stage|list>` — open-source contributor mode; stages sanitized reports under `.pose/contributions/` without leaking proprietary code; submission remains developer-adjudicated.\n - `telemetry <enable|disable|status>` — manages anonymous, opt-in platform telemetry settings.\n - `release` / `release-notes` — governed release lifecycle (`plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`, `status`, `open-next`, `backfill`) ensuring immutable release notes, manifest freezing, provider evidence importing, and reproducible release verification. A release that introduces a governance contract carries a **Compatibility** section at the top of its notes, generated from the contract registry. `release-notes` provides a compatibility alias for viewing frozen notes. The version a cut is compared with comes from `version_source` in `.pose/policy/release.json` (`{\"path\": \"VERSION\", \"kind\": \"text\"}`, or `\"kind\": \"json\"` with a `key`): a project file holding `X.Y.Z`, with an optional `v` and `-dev` suffix. Only the engine repository may omit it; every other project must declare one. Manifest and tag integrity (spec `pose-release-manifest-tag-integrity`): a manifest is read through a strict contract (supported `schema_version`, no unknown fields, clean version and paths) that recomposes `specs`, `categories`, `breaking` and `release_input_digest` from the fragments it freezes, so an adulterated projection is refused instead of normalised. `check` and `status` verify a tag from its own tree: the tag (lightweight or annotated) is peeled to its commit, and the manifest, notes, frozen fragments and version source are read as blobs of that commit, never from the working tree. A tag whose tree holds no manifest is refused whatever the working tree holds; a later commit, provider evidence or the next cycle does not make a correct cut stale; a version source that cannot be recomputed from the manifest is reported as a limit.\n \n   **Query cursors:** paginated MCP list tools (`pose_list_specs`, `pose_list_roadmaps`, `pose_list_knowledge`, `pose_list_reports`, `pose_capability_history`) continue a query over the collection it was issued for. A cursor is signed, expires after 15 minutes and is bound to its tool, project, filters and the ordered collection with its index generation; a changed collection, another query, an expired token, a version 1 token or a server restart is refused with a `cursor-*` code and the caller restarts from the first page, never continued over a changed collection. A page is at most 500 items, discloses its `generation`, `collection` and `total`, and a missing `next_cursor` does not certify anything beyond that collection (spec `pose-generation-bound-query-cursors`).\n \n@@ -1041,29 +1041,31 @@ Declare the executor and implementation baseline before dispatch. A bundle that\n \n ```json\n {\n   \"schema_version\": 1,\n   \"provenance\": {\n     \"trust\": \"executor-attested\",\n     \"implementation\": {\"principal\": \"agent:implementer\", \"execution\": \"implementation-run-1\", \"vendor\": \"vendor-a\", \"model\": \"model-a\"}\n   },\n   \"adapters\": {\n     \"reviewer\": {\"executor\": \"agent:reviewer\", \"command\": [\"reviewer\", \"--output\", \"{output}\"], \"vendor\": \"vendor-b\", \"model\": \"model-b\"}\n   }\n }\n ```\n \n Use the existing native issuer and its attestation pin. Set protected `authority_project` and `authority_audience` in review policy; a declared project identity must agree. Keep keys outside the project. Dispatch captures origin/worktree, bundle/commit/scope/kind, configuration/argv/executable, identities, brief template/body/notes, planned stdin and actual pipe-write digests, raw and retained redacted output/transcript digests and completeness. A pipe write does not prove the vendor consumed the prompt. Session references and private keys stay outside the public proof.\n \n Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then add `--apply` to publish an immutable proof under `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL is optional (no expiry when absent), positive when supplied and at most 365 days. Verify it with `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. The proof signs the captured executor declaration; it is not an independent vendor receipt or human confirmation. Failed or interrupted audit records disclose their state and `complete_review=false`. Unknown or ambiguous draft decisions also keep that flag false. A true flag describes completed delivery with one draft decision, not approval or complete typed-conclusion validation. A completed run with incomplete stdin or truncated output cannot establish a completed review. Legacy runs have no captured provenance and cannot be upgraded by signing today's configuration. `pose review run-proof` and the issuer lookups of the verdict readers ask the question the closeout asks: for a run whose scope is done, the proof as it was given (the record stands, the issuer still holds its pin; routine drift of the repository and the proof's expiry are not asked, because the expiry bounds when a proof may back a new attestation, not whether an attestation already given to a done scope stands); for an open scope, as the repository is now.\n \n Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n \n-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/8` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/8` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n \n-**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+\n+**Runs that leave work behind.** After the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own, waits for them, and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. A dispatch that ended between reserving its attempt and recording its run leaves the attempt stalled and every later dispatch of the bundle refused; `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt.\n \n **Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n \n Opt in to the verification bar with `delegated_review: true` in review policy only when preparing adoption. This live bar requires a signed run reference for agent different-actor review and refuses a prefix-only identity; it does not enable or add the future catalog capability. Non-adopting instances keep declared behavior. Under explicit executor-attested trust, normalized vendor OR model difference satisfies different-actor (vendor difference preferred); unknown baselines and the same vendor/model refuse. A machine run never satisfies mandatory-human. Catalog adoption, cutoffs/rollback and automatic conclusion application remain separate capability work.\n \n Read the same current assurance through verify, review-check, MCP and `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` names the signed executor declaration, its source, run/proof and vendor/model comparisons. Current issuer pins, project/audience/time, sealed inputs, retained artifacts and original/continuation receipts are reverified. Revocation, missing receipts, rewritten data or stale inputs remove machine assurance. Signing does not reset quotas or erase earlier rejecting runs. The origin worktree is recorded with the run, not required of the reader: a proof verifies in any checkout of the project, so a run a CI job or the Conductor performed is verified like a local one; no universal containment is claimed.\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 1f7e93c1..efb29ab8 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -407,41 +407,41 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - Frescor de assessment (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — um assessment de componente registra seu vínculo: a árvore commitada do componente, a versão do motor e o digest da matriz de validação. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reaproveita um assessment cujo vínculo não mudou e o refaz caso contrário, mostrando `assess.freshness.<componente>` com o motivo (conteúdo mudou, motor mudou, matriz mudou, mudanças não commitadas, ou registro antigo sem vínculo). Idade sozinha nunca torna um assessment stale. Um assessment ausente ou stale de um componente declarado por uma spec em andamento aparece como obrigação consultiva de `evidence` em `state --attention`; não bloqueia nada.\n - `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lista follow-ups abertos com um motivo de reconciliação: `target-terminal` (uma spec citada pelo follow-up está done, superseded ou abandoned), `evidence-present` (um teste citado agora existe), `overdue` (a data de revisão passou), além de clusters lexicais de quase-duplicatas. Cada candidato mostra sua evidência e seus limites; nada recebe disposição, similaridade nunca vira `duplicate` sem julgamento, e um follow-up aberto nunca sai do Attention sem uma disposição escrita na spec. O Attention mostra o mesmo motivo no item de dívida residual.\n - `action list --present [--actor <id|papel>] [--json]` (spec `pose-action-request-presentation`) — agrupa as solicitações que ainda esperam resposta para uma única conversa: por origem e primeira fase restringida, as que restringem start ou execution primeiro (`interrupt`), as que restringem só a release marcadas como capazes de esperar. Cada solicitação mantém seu id, digest, revisão e resposta, e mostra exatamente o conteúdo ao qual a resolução se vincula; solicitações satisfeitas não são perguntadas de novo. MCP: `pose_action_requests` com `present: true`. Decidir quando interromper uma pessoa fica com o consumidor (Harne8).\n - Superfície progressiva de spec (spec `pose-progressive-spec-surface`) — `pose new-spec <slug> --surface minimal|standard|full` registra `surface:` no frontmatter. Uma spec `minimal` mantém Intent, Requirements, o Technical Plan com Artifacts, Validation com o requirement trace e o Final Report com os follow-ups; remove a seção Tasks (local do planner) e as subseções do relatório que `pose specs facts <slug> [--json]` deriva do índice de entrega (paths dos commits atribuídos, checks que produziram evidência no escopo). `lint-spec` deixa de exigir Tasks numa spec mínima e não relaxa mais nada: os gates de trace, follow-ups e amendments continuam iguais. Os fatos nunca incluem intenção, rationale ou risco aceito. Vínculo de evidência (spec `pose-spec-facts-evidence-binding`): `specs facts` separa `checks` (resultados cujo scope provenance nomeia a spec, com a base e se rodaram contra o head dos change sets da spec) de `candidates` (resultados que o índice guarda e que nada liga à spec: módulo declarado ou `components` vazio não é provenance); candidatos são impressos como `CANDIDATE, not evidence of this spec`. `freshness` informa `fresh`, `stale` (um input mudou depois da publicação do índice), `unknown` (nunca publicado como conjunto) ou `unavailable`, e uma limitação manda rodar `pose index` quando não dá para estabelecer; ler os facts nunca reconstrói o índice nem conclui que um requisito está satisfeito.\n - **Refresh automático de project-state** (spec `pose-project-state-refresh-contract`) — um registro interno de hooks pós-evento dispara um `pose state refresh` **parcial** (apenas as seções que o evento afeta) nos pontos que o POSE já intercepta: um `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict` bem-sucedido numa spec `status: done` (`spec_closeout` → Specs & Roadmaps, Follow-ups, Validação & Evidência), `pose amend` (`spec_amend` → Specs & Roadmaps, Decisões & Conhecimento), `pose reconcile-evidence record` (`evidence_reconciled` → Validação & Evidência) e `pose assess snapshot` (`assessment_snapshot` → Capabilities). Quando o evento carrega um commit e `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` está configurado, o consumidor chama `components_hit` (spec `graphforge-components-hit-contract`) do baseline do estado até o commit do evento e anexa os componentes atingidos à seção Arquitetura (refresh **dirigido**); sem GraphForge configurado, o refresh permanece completo/não dirigido — zero acoplamento de build. Sem daemon e sem watcher de filesystem: é uma chamada síncrona no ponto exato em que cada evento já acontece. **Best-effort por padrão**: um refresh que falha nunca bloqueia o comando que disparou o evento — em vez disso marca `refresh_pending: <event>` no frontmatter do estado (limpo pelo próximo refresh bem-sucedido, qualquer que seja o gatilho). O modo estrito é opt-in por política (`strict_refresh: true`) — ali, uma falha de refresh falha o comando disparador. Toda execução (disparada ou manual) é registrada no log append-only do próprio artefato (apenas metadados: gatilho, alvo, resultado `ok|failed|skipped`, duração, hashes das seções alteradas — nunca conteúdo). Chave de dedup `hash(event+target+commit)`: o mesmo evento processado duas vezes (retry/replay) não repete o refresh, resultado `skipped`; refreshes `manual`/`ci` nunca são deduplicados entre si (uma chamada explícita sempre roda). `release_cut` está registrado no mapa evento→seções mas não tem produtor dentro do pose-mcp hoje — cortar um release pertence ao Conductor (serviço separado); o gatilho está pronto para quando essa integração existir.\n - **Gatilhos de reavaliação de capability** (spec `pose-capability-assessment-triggers`, mesmo registro de hooks do refresh automático acima) — o consumidor `assessment-staleness`, registrado em `spec_closeout`, resolve quais componentes um closeout alcançou (via `components_hit` quando `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` está configurado; fallback: interseção dos arquivos tocados pelo evento com os globs `paths:` declarados manualmente por mecanismo no assessment) e marca como stale todo mecanismo de capability afetado — nunca mutando um score, apenas registrando `since`/`trigger`/`hits` e projetando uma demanda cobrável (origem `assessment-trigger`) em `pose followups --open`, sem armazenamento duplicado. `pose assess snapshot` limpa as marcas dos mecanismos que reavalia e registra o vínculo marca→snapshot no histórico. Sob demanda: `pose assess stale [--json]` lista marcas pendentes; `pose assess request --mechanism <id> [--reason <text>]` cria uma manualmente (o mesmo caminho que uma ação de UI \"sinalizar para reavaliação\" chamaria via MCP). Ferramenta MCP: `pose_capability_stale`. Sem GraphForge e sem `paths:` declarado, o evento registra `capability_mapping_unavailable` no log de refresh — sinal visível, nada marcado em silêncio. O limiar antirruído (`min_hits`, `level` de hit `direct`/`any`, owner/SLA default da demanda) é configurável, compartilhando o mesmo arquivo de política já usado para staleness por idade/commits (opcional; ausente significa estes mesmos defaults conservadores).\n - **Governança de docs** (spec `pose-docs-governance-contract`) — contrato opt-in por projeto para documentação, mesma mecânica do assessment de capability acima: ausente, todo projeto segue válido em todo lugar. `pose docs-init [--profile library|service|cli|monorepo]` cria um manifesto declarando as raízes governadas de documentação e, por doc, `path`/`doc_type` (Diátaxis `tutorial`/`howto`/`reference`/`explanation`, ou valor customizado)/`topics`/`owns`/`applies_to`/opcionalmente `review_after`. `pose docs-check [--json] [--explain <rule>]` roda sete regras determinísticas e offline — doc declarado ausente do disco, arquivo presente mas não declarado, frontmatter mínimo faltando (`title`/`doc_type`), link relativo quebrado, referência tipada quebrada (`spec:`/`adr:`/`knowledge:`/`doc:`/...), staleness (data de review da própria entrada, ou a janela default do manifesto contada a partir do último commit que a tocou) e uma varredura de segurança reusando a mesma checagem determinística de instrução insegura/forma de segredo que as skills já rodam — cada uma com severidade configurável (`error`/`warning`/`off`). `pose check --strict` incorpora o `docs-check` quando o manifesto existe (opt-in por presença); apenas erros bloqueiam, avisos aparecem sem bloquear. `pose docs-sync` sincroniza metadados de governança. Ferramenta MCP: `pose_docs_state`. O project-state ganha uma seção Docs aditiva (presença/perfil/raízes do manifesto, contagens de declarados/não declarados/stale/erro/aviso).\n - **Gatilhos de review pendente de docs** (spec `pose-docs-assessment-followups`, terceiro consumidor do mesmo registro de hooks das duas entradas acima — reusado sem modificação) — um consumidor `docs-review`, registrado em `spec_closeout`, resolve quais componentes/arquivos um closeout alcançou (`components_hit` quando configurado, casado contra entradas `owns:` declaradas como `component:<id>`; caso contrário, os arquivos tocados pelo evento casados contra os paths/globs `owns:` de cada doc, onde um diretório como `\"site\"` cobre todo arquivo abaixo dele) e marca como review-pending todo doc cuja área declarada foi alcançada — nunca editando o doc, nunca tocando um score. As marcas se acumulam num log append-only fora do arquivo do próprio doc e projetam uma demanda sintética e com dono em `pose followups --open` (origem `docs:<caminho-do-doc>`), reusando o campo `owner` da própria entrada do manifesto quando declarado. `pose docs-review resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]` fecha de uma vez todas as marcas pendentes num doc, registrando `updated` (default, captura o commit atual) ou `no_change_needed` (motivo obrigatório); `pose docs-review request <doc>`/`--all-stale` cobrem o caminho sob demanda — o segundo transforma todo doc atualmente stale em demanda ativa numa chamada. A saída do próprio `docs-check` e o `pose_docs_state` listam aditivamente o que segue pendente. Sem mapa de componentes e sem `owns:` declarado, o evento registra um sinal visível em vez de marcar algo em silêncio — aqui o fallback por path é o caminho de força total do mecanismo (`owns:` é expresso como paths por padrão), não um caminho menor. O limiar antirruído (`min_hits`, `level` de hit, owner/SLA default) é configurável, compartilhando o mesmo formato de política dos gatilhos de capability em arquivo próprio (opcional; ausente significa estes mesmos defaults conservadores).\n - `history-check` — verifica que todo `.jsonl` em `reports/history/` está sob versionamento git. Sem isso, `recurrence-check` e `stats` divergem entre máquinas. Strict bloqueia; tolerant avisa.\n - `suggest` — lê [`task-map.json`](.pose/indexes/task-map.json) e imprime a trilha canônica (workflow + skill + rules + spec/ADR + knowledge) para um tipo de tarefa. Sem argumentos, lista todos os tipos. `--domain <d>` aplica rules adicionais por domínio (frontend, backend-go, k8s); `--path <p>` infere o domínio por heurísticas e via [`repo-map.json`](.pose/indexes/repo-map.json) (`language` → frontend/backend-go); `--json` para consumo por agentes. Domínios (spec `pose-distributed-instructions-parity`): `--domain` e o domínio inferido de `--path` passam por uma taxonomia (`go`/`golang`/`backend-go` → `backend-go`; `react`/`frontend-react`/`frontend` → `frontend`; `kubernetes`/`helm`/`k8s` → `k8s`), o JSON expõe `domain_input` e `domain_source: alias`, e um domínio sem regras no task-map é reportado (`domain_status`) em vez de tomar emprestado o de outro; um módulo `backend` só é o domínio Go quando sua linguagem é Go. `rules_status` (e a saída humana) diz de cada regra se está `installed` (com o path), `extension-needed` (com `pose extension install <id>`) ou `unavailable`; suggest não anuncia arquivo de regra que não está no disco e não instala nada.\n - `stats` — agrega outcomes do history JSONL por workflow, task ou context. Habilita decisões objetivas (promover check de optional → required, identificar workflows instáveis, comparar ci vs manual). `--since-days N` filtra a janela; `--json` para consumo por máquina.\n - `stats governance` — projeção read-only schema 2 sobre histórico de reports, bundles de review selados, atestações e declarações explícitas de remediação. Mantém preparação, julgamento, intervenção, frescor, telemetria, proveniência e cobertura independentes, nunca emite score de qualidade e explicita dados ausentes ou inválidos. `--report-type` filtra tentativas de validação; `--band` filtra reviews e vínculos de findings. A identidade de replay é `(report_type, task_slug, sequence)`; linhas sem identidade permanecem visíveis na cobertura. MCP `pose_governance_stats` aceita os mesmos seletores e exige `project_id` para escolher uma raiz federada. Vínculos explícitos incluem apenas IDs opacos, slugs, categoria e banda de origem, limitados a 100 linhas com truncamento indicado.\n - `stats governance --waits --rework` (spec `pose-governance-wait-rework-observability`) — duas dimensões a mais ao lado dos resultados, nunca somadas num score. Com `--waits` ou `--rework`, só as dimensões pedidas são calculadas; `--outcomes` acrescenta os resultados, que leem todo o histórico. `--waits` cronometra as action requests e mantém três medidas separadas: a idade de cada solicitação, o intervalo atribuído à espera por um ator ou sistema externo, e `known_blocking_seconds`, a união por spec dos intervalos em que uma solicitação restringiu a execução da spec inteira (solicitações simultâneas contam uma vez). Nenhuma delas é inatividade; timestamps ausentes contam como desconhecidos. `--rework` classifica bundles selados substituídos pelo que mudou (sujeito, intenção ou plano, plano de review, evidência, ou desconhecido) e atestações extras num bundle por divergência de review ou atestação anterior inválida contra a política atual, deixando outras causas como desconhecidas. `scripts/bench-governance.sh [runs] [pose-binary]` cronometra `state --attention`, `check --strict` e `index` na mesma revisão e reporta tempo e tamanho do índice separadamente, com a revisão do corpus e a versão do motor.\n - Validade de premissas (spec `pose-assumption-validity-scope`) — uma `Premissa A<N>` material pode declarar `Escopo de validade:` (o contexto em que vale) e entradas `Gatilho de obsolescência:` `<kind>:<value>@<pin>`, onde kind é `doc`, `report`, `knowledge`, `adr` ou `snapshot` e o pin é um prefixo (8+ hex) do sha256 do conteúdo contra o qual a premissa foi julgada. `lint-spec --design-check` reporta um gatilho sem pin com o pin a acrescentar, um ilegível como desconhecido, e um gatilho de calendário (`date:`, `ttl:`, …) como não avaliado: validade é por contexto, não por expiração, e premissas triviais não precisam de nenhum campo. Quando o conteúdo fixado muda, a projeção de obrigações (produtor `premises`) emite uma obrigação de julgamento `premise-stale` para specs draft e in-progress, advisory no review e com escopo na premissa e nas decisões baseadas nela. A evidência registrada permanece; é descrita como julgada contra o conteúdo fixado e não válida para o atual, e nada é invalidado automaticamente. Contratos de provedores ou remotos não são monitorados pelo core. O estado do gatilho é só projeção e nunca muda o digest do design.\n - Observação de falsificador (spec `pose-falsifier-reconsideration`) — uma `Decisão D<N>` material pode acrescentar `Efeito esperado:` e `Verificação do falsificador: check:<module>/<check>`, nomeando o check de validação indexado cuja falha a contradiz. Quando o índice de entrega contém um resultado `fail` desse check, a projeção de obrigações (produtor `falsifiers`) emite uma obrigação de julgamento `falsifier-observed`, advisory no review e com escopo na decisão, para qualquer spec que não esteja superseded ou abandoned (decisões continuam valendo depois de done). O motor não infere causa nem julga arquitetura: a falha é uma observação, a decisão e seu rationale nunca são editados, e uma pessoa registra por que ela ainda vale ou abre uma decisão substituta. Um resultado aprovado ou ausente não gera nada. `lint-spec --design-check` avisa sobre referência malformada e sobre verificação de falsificador sem falsificador declarado.\n - `migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]` (spec `pose-v7-legacy-cleanup-plan`) — inventário somente leitura do que uma futura major converteria, manteria ou recusaria: layouts de spec, specs `blocked`, chaves legadas e desconhecidas da política de review, bundles selados, atestações por modo de identidade e atribuição, e transferências incompletas do lado deste projeto, cada um com sua ação e critério de remoção. Não escreve nada. Bundles selados, atestações e assinaturas nunca são reescritos. `--apply` é recusado com o fato atual: não há apply neste motor, o único modo é `--dry-run`, que não escreve nada; nenhum apply futuro é prometido.\n - `assess design --spec <slug>` — projeção read-only e bounded sobre o subject canônico selado de review. Observa deltas de dependência, componente, metadata de delivery, contratos de governança e contratos públicos, mantém coverage unknown/unsupported explícita e nunca calcula score de complexidade ou arquitetura. O equivalente MCP é `pose_design_delta`.\n - `usage` — agrega automaticamente eventos locais e por projeto de uso da CLI e do MCP: chamadas, outcomes de execução/semântica, latência, findings observados e ciclo de vida estável (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agentes nunca mantêm contadores. Os eventos são best-effort, somente locais e ficam fora da árvore versionada; argumentos, saída, paths, identidade de projeto/usuário e IDs crus de findings nunca são persistidos. `--since-days 0` inclui todo o histórico; filtre com `--tool` ou `--surface`; equivalente MCP: `pose_usage`. É evidência de uso do produto, não outcome DORA nem score individual de produtividade.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingerem eventos de entrega schema v2 sem identidade individual e calculam métricas para um ambiente de produção explícito (`--environment`, padrão `production`). Deployments exigem `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidentes exigem ambiente; recovery inclui apenas incidentes resolvidos com `--caused-by-deployment`. `adoption-metrics` agrega taxas de especificações, roadmaps e validação de ciclo de vida.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — inspeção de catálogo read-only e offline (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Casa entradas de diretório contra o catálogo mantido de perfis (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — e **.NET**), reportando por perfil: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (múltiplos managers presentes resolvem por prioridade declarada), `confidence` (`medium` sob conflito) e se a ferramenta nativa pré-requisito está no `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, nunca executando um arquivo do projeto. Markers detectados alimentam `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` do mesmo jeito que Node/Go/Rust/Java já fazem; os checks propriamente ditos rodam pelas stacks `python`/`dotnet` da [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json). A prioridade de manager Python é expressa com `when.fileNotExistsAny` (pular quando existir qualquer lockfile/marker de prioridade maior) ao lado dos predicados `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` já existentes.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities. `discover`, `integrate` e `tech-debt` aceitam `--no-persist` para imprimir o resultado sem gravar `.pose/assessments/` nem `.pose/state/`; no checkout selado de uma review delegada (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) eles nunca gravam, `init`, `snapshot` e `request` recusam, e o plano de review os roda com `--no-persist`, para que um revisor que roda um assessment planejado não derrube o próprio run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — diagnósticos de ambiente, dependências, runtime nativo e saúde da instância POSE, além de governança que só falharia mais adiante: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (um profile selecionado exige uma classe que nenhum check pode emitir), `validate.class-producers` (um critério aceita apenas classes que nenhum check registrado produz), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks sem `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set` e `<policy>.policy-keys` para nove políticas (chaves que o motor não lê, ou seja, uma configuração com erro de digitação que silenciosamente não faz nada).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — manutenção idempotente (listar/arquivar/expurgar). Mutações exigem `--apply`. O housekeeping de reports **nunca toca em `history/`**: o JSONL é a fonte de verdade para `recurrence-check` e comparações temporais de `report`. Defaults: stale = 120d, purga de arquivo = 365d.\n - `amend` — histórico append-only de emendas da spec (`amendments.jsonl` ao lado do `spec.md` de uma spec em pasta, ou `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` ao lado de uma spec flat datada; a spec é resolvida pelo store, então os dois layouts recebem emendas do mesmo modo). `--baseline` fotografa o hash de cada R-ID; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` reconhece uma mudança material; `--list` renderiza o histórico e os reconhecimentos pendentes. Em specs `done` com histórico, o `lint-spec` rejeita todo requisito cujo texto atual não esteja reconhecido por um evento — specs não podem ser reescritas em silêncio depois da evidência. `--nodes [--json]` mostra a projeção versionada de contract nodes: requisitos, premissas e decisões com namespace, hash de conteúdo, estado e relações. Com `contract_nodes_version: 1` adotado na policy de review, `--baseline` grava um snapshot R/A/D schema 2, `--ids` aceita A/D, `--change transition` reconhece uma mudança de estado permitida, `editorial` é recusado quando o nó mudou estado ou relações, e o gate também rejeita decisão ativa apoiada em premissa invalidada ou retirada. Sem a capability o gate segue só com requisitos e um evento schema 2 é recusado; logs schema 1 mantêm o significado.\n - `start` — início atômico de uma spec draft, registrado num start record por spec. Sem flags é um preview somente leitura vinculado a digest: readiness e dependências, as obrigações de review declaradas (sem exigir structural delta futuro) e a baseline R/A/D. `--apply --digest <sha256>` grava essa baseline e move a spec de `draft` para `in-progress` com lock por spec e compare-and-swap; exige `atomic_start_version: 1` na policy de review, repete de forma idempotente, retoma uma execução interrompida e recusa outro plano para spec já iniciada. `--cancel` desfaz um start que não moveu a spec. A trava por spec registra seu dono (host, digests dos IDs de máquina e de boot, namespace de PID, PID e início do processo); uma trava de outra chamada é informada, nunca quebrada pela idade, como `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` ou `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Morte só é provada na mesma máquina Linux — outro boot, ou um PID que não roda ou agora é de outro processo — e `--recover-lock <digest do dono>`, com o digest que `--status` mostra, remove essa trava só se os bytes e o dono morto não mudaram, deixando o start record para retomar; um dono desconhecido (outro host, a trava vazia de um motor anterior, outra plataforma) é verificado e removido por uma pessoa. A mesma trava é a trava de ciclo de vida da spec: close e transfer também a tomam, então dois deles nunca se intercalam numa spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifica cada nó como `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` ou `legacy-unbaselined`, recalcula as obrigações e, com a capability, pede reconciliação quando status ou baseline registrada foram editados à mão. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limita a capability às specs criadas a partir dessa data: uma spec mais antiga em andamento sem start aparece como `legacy-unbaselined` com uma nota e não bloqueia nada; sem a data, toda spec assim exige reconciliação. Um start registra precedência observável, não quando algo foi decidido; o MCP expõe a visão somente leitura como `pose_start_status`.\n - `knowledge-usage` — projeta as citações `knowledge:<slug>` das specs por artefato (dono, expiração, specs citantes). Sinais de uso informam a review do dono; o TTL nunca é estendido automaticamente. Refs `knowledge:` órfãs falham no `knowledge-check`.\n - `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — ranqueamento léxico determinístico e projeções consultivas para conhecimento, agrupamento de feedback, marcos de portfólio multi-roadmap e reconciliação de evidências de validação. Sugestões nunca bloqueiam nem se autoaplicam sem confirmação.\n - `hooks` — gerencia symlinks do binário nativo em `.git/hooks/`. O nome de invocação seleciona `check --tolerant` para `pre-commit` e `index` para `post-merge`; `install --force` preserva backup de hooks preexistentes. Uma instância que roda o POSE pelo próprio CLI vinculado a uma revisão declara um executor num arquivo `hooks.json` de `.pose/policy/` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"runner\":\"<executável relativo ao projeto>\"}`): o hook passa a rodar o executor com o nome do hook e devolve o código de saída dele, e `hooks status` o nomeia; um executor fora do projeto, não executável ou com outro schema é recusado e nada roda.\n - `version` — exibe versão compilada do binário Go, SHA do commit e compara a versão do schema do repositório com os requisitos do motor.\n - `context` — resolve um projeto lógico selecionado e uma tarefa opcional, local ou qualificada por `xref:`, pelo resolvedor compartilhado de artefatos. O resultado JSON traz a autoridade canônica, as revisões do projeto e do artefato, o estado de resolução, os contratos suportados e um `context_revision`; nunca inclui roots do filesystem. Use esse token para detectar checkout ou revisão alterados antes de uma escrita entre projetos.\n - Escritas CLI entre projetos exigem o destino em `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` e o `context_revision` de uma chamada recente de `pose context`. Use `new-spec-qualified` para criação qualificada, de modo que motores antigos recusem o próprio comando. Um projeto encontrado apenas por varredura de `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` pode ser lido, mas não concede acesso de escrita. Specs canônicas existentes são reutilizadas; redirecionamentos e transferências incompletas bloqueiam a criação de uma cópia.\n - `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — instala o runtime, regras, workflows, templates e documentação embutidos do POSE em um diretório de destino sem clonar, grava no `adopted_at` da política de changelog o dia em que a instância a recebeu e roda o gate estrito.\n - `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — importa especificações externas para o formato canônico do POSE com frontmatter e 7 seções padronizadas.\n - `serve-mcp` — inicia o servidor POSE Model Context Protocol via stdio (`--stdio`) ou HTTP para integração com agentes de IA. O servidor stdio encerra com SIGTERM; ele resolve o CLI `pose` que executa uma única vez, ao iniciar, então um servidor iniciado antes de um `pose update` continua funcionando com o binário atualizado.\n - `report-limitation` — registra limitação, defeito ou sugestão de melhoria do motor em `.pose/feedback/` com opção de submissão (`--submit`).\n - `contribute <enable|disable|status|stage|list>` — modo contribuidor open-source; registra relatórios sanitizados sob `.pose/contributions/` sem vazar código privado; a submissão permanece a critério do desenvolvedor.\n - `release` / `release-notes` — ciclo de vida governado de releases (`plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`, `status`, `open-next`, `backfill`) garantindo notas de release imutáveis, congelamento de manifesto, importação de evidências de provedor e verificação reproduzível. Uma release que introduz um contrato de governança traz uma seção **Compatibility** no topo das notas, gerada a partir do registro de contratos. `release-notes` fornece alias de compatibilidade para visualização de notas congeladas. A versão com que o corte é comparado vem de `version_source` em `.pose/policy/release.json` (`{\"path\": \"VERSION\", \"kind\": \"text\"}`, ou `\"kind\": \"json\"` com `key`): um arquivo do projeto com `X.Y.Z`, com `v` e sufixo `-dev` opcionais. Só o repositório do motor pode omiti-lo; todo outro projeto precisa declarar um. Integridade de manifest e tag (spec `pose-release-manifest-tag-integrity`): o manifest é lido por um contrato estrito (`schema_version` suportado, sem campos desconhecidos, versão e paths limpos) que recompõe `specs`, `categories`, `breaking` e `release_input_digest` a partir dos fragments que congela, de modo que uma projeção adulterada é recusada em vez de normalizada. `check` e `status` verificam uma tag pela sua própria árvore: a tag (leve ou anotada) é resolvida até o commit, e manifest, notas, fragments congelados e fonte de versão são lidos como blobs desse commit, nunca do worktree. Uma tag cuja árvore não tem manifest é recusada qualquer que seja o worktree; um commit posterior, evidência do provider ou o próximo ciclo não tornam obsoleto um cut correto; uma fonte de versão que não pode ser recomputada a partir do manifest é reportada como limite.\n \n   **Cursores de consulta:** as tools MCP de listagem paginadas (`pose_list_specs`, `pose_list_roadmaps`, `pose_list_knowledge`, `pose_list_reports`, `pose_capability_history`) continuam uma consulta sobre a coleção para a qual o cursor foi emitido. O cursor é assinado, expira em 15 minutos e está ligado à tool, ao projeto, aos filtros e à coleção ordenada com a geração do índice; coleção alterada, outra consulta, token expirado, token versão 1 ou reinício do servidor são recusados com um código `cursor-*` e quem chama recomeça da primeira página, sem nunca continuar sobre uma coleção alterada. Uma página tem no máximo 500 itens, divulga `generation`, `collection` e `total`, e a ausência de `next_cursor` não certifica nada além dessa coleção (spec `pose-generation-bound-query-cursors`).\n \n ### Contrato de conexão MCP\n@@ -1051,29 +1051,31 @@ Declare executor e baseline de implementação antes do dispatch. Um bundle que\n \n ```json\n {\n   \"schema_version\": 1,\n   \"provenance\": {\n     \"trust\": \"executor-attested\",\n     \"implementation\": {\"principal\": \"agent:implementer\", \"execution\": \"implementation-run-1\", \"vendor\": \"vendor-a\", \"model\": \"model-a\"}\n   },\n   \"adapters\": {\n     \"reviewer\": {\"executor\": \"agent:reviewer\", \"command\": [\"reviewer\", \"--output\", \"{output}\"], \"vendor\": \"vendor-b\", \"model\": \"model-b\"}\n   }\n }\n ```\n \n Reutilize o issuer nativo e seu pin de atestações. Configure `authority_project` e `authority_audience` protegidos na policy de review; a identidade declarada do projeto deve concordar. Mantenha chaves fora do projeto. O dispatch captura origem/worktree, bundle/commit/scope/kind, configuração/argv/executável, identidades, template/body/notas do brief, stdin planejado e bytes escritos no pipe, digests de saída/transcript brutos e retidos com redação, além de completude. Escrever no pipe não prova que o fornecedor consumiu o prompt. Sessões e chaves privadas ficam fora da prova pública.\n \n Visualize `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <nome> [--ttl <duração>]` e acrescente `--apply` para publicar uma prova imutável em `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL é opcional (sem expiração quando ausente), positivo quando informado e limitado a 365 dias. Verifique com `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. A prova assina a declaração capturada do executor; não é recibo independente do fornecedor nem confirmação humana. Auditorias failed/interrupted mostram seu estado e `complete_review=false`. Decisão desconhecida ou ambígua também mantém a flag falsa. A flag verdadeira descreve entrega concluída com uma decisão de draft, não aprovação nem validação completa da conclusão tipada. Run completed com stdin incompleto ou saída truncada não estabelece review concluído. Runs legados sem proveniência capturada não são promovidos assinando a configuração atual. `pose review run-proof` e as buscas do emissor dos leitores de veredito fazem a pergunta que o fechamento faz: para uma run cujo escopo está done, a prova como foi dada (o registro se sustenta, o emissor mantém o pin; a deriva rotineira do repositório e a expiração da prova não são perguntadas, porque a expiração limita quando uma prova pode amparar uma nova atestação, não se uma atestação já dada a um escopo done se mantém); para um escopo aberto, como o repositório está agora.\n \n Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicitação completa de `pose review attest`. O issuer deriva do run os vínculos de principal/execução e assina a referência exata. Critérios, tools, evidências e preflight continuam obrigatórios. Draft negativo ou ambíguo não apoia aprovação; a atestação não pode anteceder o término do run ou sua prova assinada. Provas de adjudication/smoke vinculam seus kinds e não servem como atestações de review. Validar/aplicar automaticamente conclusões completas e tipadas pertence à capability, não à assinatura do run.\n \n-**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/8` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/8` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n+**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n \n-**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+\n+**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** Depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria, espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. Um envio que terminou entre reservar a tentativa e registrar o run deixa a tentativa parada e todo envio seguinte do bundle recusado; `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo.\n \n **Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\n \n Ative a barreira de verificação com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review somente ao preparar adoção. Essa barreira viva exige prova de run para review de agente different-actor e recusa prefixo isolado; ela não adiciona nem habilita a capability futura no catálogo. Instâncias não adotantes mantêm o comportamento declared. Com confiança executor-attested explícita, vendor OU modelo normalizado diferente satisfaz different-actor (vendor distinto preferido); baseline desconhecida e mesmo vendor/modelo recusam. Run de máquina nunca satisfaz mandatory-human. Adoção no catálogo, cutoffs/rollback e aplicação automática de conclusões continuam no escopo separado da capability.\n \n Leia a mesma assurance atual por verify, review-check, MCP e `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` identifica a declaração assinada, sua fonte, run/proof e comparação de vendor/modelo. Pins atuais, projeto/audience/tempo, inputs selados, artefatos retidos e receipts originais/de continuação são revalidados. Revogação, receipt ausente, dados reescritos ou inputs stale retiram a assurance de máquina. Assinar não restaura quota nem apaga runs negativos anteriores. O worktree de origem fica registrado com o run, não é exigido de quem lê: uma prova verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto, então um run feito por um job de CI ou pelo Conductor é verificado como um local; não há alegação de contenção universal.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go | sed -n '1,260p' && nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go | sed -n '1,180p' && nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go | sed -n '297,333p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go | sed -n '1,260p' && nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go | sed -n '1,180p' && nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go | sed -n '297,333p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t//go:build linux\n     2\t\n     3\tpackage cli\n     4\t\n     5\timport (\n     6\t\t\"os\"\n     7\t\t\"os/exec\"\n     8\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n     9\t\t\"strconv\"\n    10\t\t\"strings\"\n    11\t\t\"syscall\"\n    12\t\t\"testing\"\n    13\t\t\"time\"\n    14\t\n    15\t\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n    16\t)\n    17\t\n    18\t// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1, R2, R4: a\n    19\t// reviewer that ends while a command it started in a session of its own is\n    20\t// still running leaves a run that completes, with that command ended before\n    21\t// the cleanup and named in the run record.\n    22\tfunc TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning(t *testing.T) {\n    23\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"setsid\"); err != nil {\n    24\t\t\tt.Skip(\"setsid is not available\")\n    25\t\t}\n    26\t\troot := dispatchInstance(t)\n    27\t\tpidFile := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"leftover.pid\")\n    28\t\tscript := `cat >/dev/null; setsid sh -c 'echo $$ > \"` + pidFile + `\"; while :; do touch \"$TMPDIR/leftover\" 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.05; done' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & ` +\n    29\t\t\t`i=0; while [ ! -s \"` + pidFile + `\" ] && [ $i -lt 100 ]; do sleep 0.05; i=$((i+1)); done; printf 'decision: approved\\n'`\n    30\t\twriteReviewers(t, root, map[string]pose.ReviewerAdapter{\"fake\": {Command: []string{\"sh\", \"-c\", script}, Vendor: \"fixture\", Model: \"fixture\", Containment: &pose.ReviewerContainment{SchemaVersion: 1, Profile: \"trusted-process\"}}})\n    31\t\tcode, out := runPose(t, root, \"delegate\", \"review\", \"spec:alpha\", \"--agent\", \"fake\")\n    32\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(pidFile)\n    33\t\tif err != nil {\n    34\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the leftover never started: %v (%d %s)\", err, code, out)\n    35\t\t}\n    36\t\tpid, _ := strconv.Atoi(strings.TrimSpace(string(raw)))\n    37\t\tt.Cleanup(func() { _ = syscall.Kill(pid, syscall.SIGKILL) })\n    38\t\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(2 * time.Second)\n    39\t\tfor syscall.Kill(pid, 0) == nil && time.Now().Before(deadline) {\n    40\t\t\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n    41\t\t\t\tbreak\n    42\t\t\t}\n    43\t\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n    44\t\t}\n    45\t\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && !strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n    46\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the process the reviewer left running survived the run: %s\", stat)\n    47\t\t}\n    48\t\truns, _, _ := (pose.Store{Root: root}).ReadReviewRuns(\"\")\n    49\t\tif len(runs) != 1 {\n    50\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"runs: %d (%d %s)\", len(runs), code, out)\n    51\t\t}\n    52\t\trun := runs[0]\n    53\t\tif run.Status != pose.ReviewRunCompleted || run.Safety == nil || run.Safety.Operation.Outcome != pose.OutcomeCompleted {\n    54\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run did not complete with its cleanup: status=%s failure=%s safety=%+v\", run.Status, run.Failure, run.Safety)\n    55\t\t}\n    56\t\tif !strings.Contains(strings.Join(run.Safety.Limitations, \"\\n\"), \"process(es) the run left running\") {\n    57\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run record does not name the ended process: %v\", run.Safety.Limitations)\n    58\t\t}\n    59\t}\n     1\t//go:build linux\n     2\t\n     3\tpackage pose\n     4\t\n     5\timport (\n     6\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n     7\t\t\"errors\"\n     8\t\t\"os\"\n     9\t\t\"os/exec\"\n    10\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n    11\t\t\"strings\"\n    12\t\t\"testing\"\n    13\t\t\"time\"\n    14\t)\n    15\t\n    16\t// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R5, R6: a dispatch\n    17\t// that ended between its reservation and its run record leaves an attempt\n    18\t// that blocks the bundle; once its owner is proven gone, cancel releases it\n    19\t// with a failed run and its receipt, and the ledger reads again.\n    20\tfunc TestAnAbandonedAttemptIsReleasedWithARecord(t *testing.T) {\n    21\t\troot := t.TempDir()\n    22\t\ts := Store{Root: root}\n    23\t\tbundle := \"rvb-00000000000000a1\"\n    24\t\ta := DelegatedReviewAttempt{SchemaVersion: 1, BundleID: bundle, BundleDigest: \"sha256:\" + strings.Repeat(\"a\", 64), Scope: \"spec:alpha\", Adapter: \"fake\", Ordinal: 1,\n    25\t\t\tReservedAt: time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano), Nonce: strings.Repeat(\"0\", 32)}\n    26\t\ta.AttemptID = attemptIdentity(a)\n    27\t\trel, _ := filepath.Rel(root, reviewAttemptPath(root, a))\n    28\t\tif err := publishLedgerJSON(root, filepath.ToSlash(rel), a); err != nil {\n    29\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    30\t\t}\n    31\t\t// The ledger refuses the bundle while the reservation has no receipt.\n    32\t\tif _, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(a); err == nil || !strings.Contains(err.Error(), \"incomplete or unreadable\") {\n    33\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unfinished reservation read as finished: %v\", err)\n    34\t\t}\n    35\t\n    36\t\t// The marker the interrupted dispatch left: its owner is a process that\n    37\t\t// has exited.\n    38\t\tdead := exec.Command(\"true\")\n    39\t\tif err := dead.Run(); err != nil {\n    40\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    41\t\t}\n    42\t\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + a.AttemptID, Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n    43\t\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n    44\t\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n    45\t\towner.PID = dead.Process.Pid\n    46\t\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: a.AttemptID, RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"b\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: a.Scope, BundleID: bundle,\n    47\t\t\tStartedAt: a.ReservedAt, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: a.ReservedAt, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n    48\t\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n    49\t\tif err != nil {\n    50\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    51\t\t}\n    52\t\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n    53\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, a.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n    54\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    55\t\t}\n    56\t\n    57\t\trun, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(a.AttemptID, time.Now())\n    58\t\tif err != nil {\n    59\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    60\t\t}\n    61\t\tif run.Status != ReviewRunFailed || !strings.Contains(run.Failure, \"abandoned\") {\n    62\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the release recorded %s: %s\", run.Status, run.Failure)\n    63\t\t}\n    64\t\tif _, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(a); err != nil {\n    65\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the released attempt still blocks the bundle: %v\", err)\n    66\t\t}\n    67\t\tif left, _ := filepath.Glob(filepath.Join(dir, a.AttemptID+\"*\")); len(left) != 0 {\n    68\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the marker or its stall claim stayed: %v\", left)\n    69\t\t}\n    70\t\t// Released once: a second release finds nothing abandoned.\n    71\t\tif _, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(a.AttemptID, time.Now()); !errors.Is(err, ErrAttemptNotAbandoned) {\n    72\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a released attempt was released again: %v\", err)\n    73\t\t}\n    74\t}\n    75\t\n    76\t// An attempt whose owner is alive is never released.\n    77\tfunc TestARunningAttemptIsNotReleased(t *testing.T) {\n    78\t\troot := t.TempDir()\n    79\t\ts := Store{Root: root}\n    80\t\tbundle := \"rvb-00000000000000a2\"\n    81\t\t// Silent for an hour, so only the owner's liveness keeps it running.\n    82\t\told := time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)\n    83\t\ta := DelegatedReviewAttempt{SchemaVersion: 1, BundleID: bundle, BundleDigest: \"sha256:\" + strings.Repeat(\"a\", 64), Scope: \"spec:alpha\", Adapter: \"fake\", Ordinal: 1, ReservedAt: old, Nonce: strings.Repeat(\"0\", 32)}\n    84\t\ta.AttemptID = attemptIdentity(a)\n    85\t\trel, _ := filepath.Rel(root, reviewAttemptPath(root, a))\n    86\t\tif err := publishLedgerJSON(root, filepath.ToSlash(rel), a); err != nil {\n    87\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    88\t\t}\n    89\t\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + a.AttemptID, Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n    90\t\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n    91\t\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n    92\t\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: a.AttemptID, RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"d\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: \"spec:alpha\", BundleID: bundle, StartedAt: old, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: old, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n    93\t\tdir, _ := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n    94\t\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n    95\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n    96\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n    97\t\t}\n    98\t\tif _, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(marker.AttemptID, time.Now()); !errors.Is(err, ErrAttemptNotAbandoned) {\n    99\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a running attempt was released: %v\", err)\n   100\t\t}\n   101\t}\n   297\t// A child that escaped the process group but keeps the run's environment is\n   298\t// ended with the run, and the guarantee is disclosed as environment-marked,\n   299\t// never as available (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes).\n   300\tfunc TestAdapterContainmentEscapedChildIsEndedWhileEnvironmentMarked(t *testing.T) {\n   301\t\tif runtime.GOOS != \"linux\" {\n   302\t\t\tt.Skip(\"Linux setsid limitation fixture\")\n   303\t\t}\n   304\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"setsid\"); err != nil {\n   305\t\t\tt.Skip(\"setsid unavailable for explicit escape fixture\")\n   306\t\t}\n   307\t\toutside := t.TempDir()\n   308\t\ttrigger := filepath.Join(outside, \"trigger\")\n   309\t\tescaped := filepath.Join(outside, \"escaped\")\n   310\t\tready := filepath.Join(outside, \"ready\")\n   311\t\tt.Cleanup(func() { _ = os.WriteFile(trigger, []byte(\"release finite child\"), 0600) })\n   312\t\tf := provenanceRunFixture(t, \"fixture-vendor\", \"fixture-model\", func(a *ReviewerAdapter) {\n   313\t\t\ta.Containment = containmentPolicy()\n   314\t\t\ta.Command = []string{\"sh\", \"-c\", `cat >/dev/null; setsid sh -c 'printf ready > \"$3\"; n=0; while test \"$n\" -lt 200; do if test -e \"$1\"; then printf escaped > \"$2\"; exit 0; fi; n=$((n+1)); sleep 0.01; done' sh \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" >/dev/null 2>&1 & n=0; while ! test -e \"$3\"; do n=$((n+1)); test \"$n\" -lt 100 || exit 2; sleep 0.01; done; printf 'decision: approved\\n'`, \"sh\", trigger, escaped, ready}\n   315\t\t})\n   316\t\tif f.run.Status != ReviewRunCompleted || f.run.Safety.Capabilities[\"escaped-descendant-termination\"] != \"environment-marked\" {\n   317\t\t\tt.Fatal(\"escaped-process termination was not disclosed as environment-marked\", f.run.Failure, f.run.Safety)\n   318\t\t}\n   319\t\tif _, err := os.Stat(escaped); !os.IsNotExist(err) {\n   320\t\t\tt.Fatal(\"escape fixture ran before dispatch returned\", err)\n   321\t\t}\n   322\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(trigger, []byte(\"release finite child\"), 0600); err != nil {\n   323\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n   324\t\t}\n   325\t\ttime.Sleep(500 * time.Millisecond)\n   326\t\tif _, err := os.Stat(escaped); !os.IsNotExist(err) {\n   327\t\t\tt.Fatal(\"the escaped child outlived the run\", err)\n   328\t\t}\n   329\t}\n   330\t\n   331\t// Spec pose-review-cleanup-removes-go-build-leftovers: the Go build directory\n   332\t// an interrupted reviewer test left in the runtime directory is removed even\n   333\t// when it alone exceeds the runtime inventory, and a link named like one is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
