{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"if [ -f .pose/rules/backend-go.md ]; then cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md; else printf 'backend-go rule is not installed\\\\n'; fi && printf '\\\\n--- relevant prior handoff ---\\\\n' && cat .pose/knowledge/2026-10-27-handoff-delegation-mcp-round5.md && printf '\\\\n--- delegation observation handoff ---\\\\n' && cat .pose/knowledge/2026-10-26-handoff-delegation-observation-round4.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n\n--- relevant prior handoff ---\n---\ntype: handoff\nslug: delegation-mcp-round5\nowner: @pose-maintainers\nsensitivity: public-internal\ncreated_at: 2026-10-27\nlast_reviewed_at: 2026-10-27\nexpires_at: 2026-11-26\nsource_refs:\n  spec: pose-delegation-mcp\n  workflow: .pose/workflows/review.md\n  commands:\n    - pose review verify spec:pose-delegation-mcp\n    - pose delegate ls --json\n    - pose validate --strict\n  external_sources: []  # [{url: \"\", accessed_at: \"YYYY-MM-DD\"}]\n---\n\n# Handoff: delegation MCP review residual risks\n\n## Context\n\nIndependent round 5 reviewed implementation f0514d01404e7bed0cfc602f2db065f3e0c6289e of spec pose-delegation-mcp. The maintainer-authorized reviewer agent:independent-gpt-6.1-sol-review declares Codex/OpenAI, another vendor than implementer Claude/Anthropic. Identity is declared; this instance uses unsigned attestations. Last reviewed: 2026-10-27. Native global strict validation passed 158/158 checks at that implementation head.\n\n## Current state\n\nAll blockers from rounds 1–4 are fixed in the reproduced cases, including B07 task continuation retries with and without --record. F08 once-only handback cleanup is fixed. Two low residual findings remain: F02 returns attempt/supervisor identity initially and resolves run identity through wait; F03 allows detached execution to survive transport cancellation between reservation and handback, potentially leaving the caller without a response.\n\nThe attempt handle remains usable, continuation retains its attempt and ledger, and cancellation does not bypass budgets or create an approval. D4 defers starting tasks over MCP, while resume/handoff of existing tasks are delivered. D3 resolves an authorized unambiguous project using the common project-scope contract.\n\n## Next checks\n\nFor F02, start or continue a synthetic run through MCP: observe attempt in the immediate response and run in wait. Align the public identity contract or allocate run identity at handback, with regressions for retries. For F03, cancel the invoking process after the active marker is visible and before handback; inspect ls/status, then wait for the same attempt to finish. Document recovery, correlation and checking the ledger before retrying. A transport cancellation is not delegate cancel. Re-review by 2026-11-26 or before changing handback identity/retry behavior.\n\n## Risks\n\nF02 is an initial presentation limitation, not loss of the run. F03 is an operational recovery window in intentional detached execution; the run is discoverable and explicit cancellation remains available. Fake-agent tests establish the engine contract, not reviewer cognition or a provider receipt. Neither finding authorizes repeated execution without the ledger's required reason or turns task/partial output into governance evidence.\n\n## Next owner\n\n@oseiaspereira88 owns F02 and F03, both low; review 2026-11-26. The institutional handoff owner is @pose-maintainers. No upstream contribution draft, issue or PR was created.\n\n## References\n\nSpec: .pose/specs/2026-10-26-pose-delegation-mcp.md. Consumed knowledge: knowledge:delegation-observation-round4, knowledge:delegation-routing-round3 and knowledge:delegated-rounds-legacy-status. Canonical validation: .pose/results/delivery-validation.json. Local probes, seven mutations and reports: .review-work/round5/ (deliberately untracked). Native bundle and attestation IDs are recorded in the spec Final Report after closeout.\n\n--- delegation observation handoff ---\n---\ntype: handoff\nslug: delegation-observation-round4\nowner: @oseiaspereira88\nsensitivity: public-internal\ncreated_at: 2026-10-26\nlast_reviewed_at: 2026-10-26\nexpires_at: 2026-11-25\nsource_refs:\n  spec: pose-delegated-review-completion-signal\n  workflow: .pose/workflows/review.md\n  commands:\n    - pose review verify spec:pose-delegated-review-completion-signal\n  external_sources: []\n---\n\n# Handoff: observation review residual follow-ups\n\n## Context\n\nIndependent round 4 of commit a8ce8cf97acb89815cc8440f028ec319c63ec1c3 closes the milestone `observation` of the roadmap `delegation`. The reviewer is the maintainer-authorized declared identity `agent:independent-claude-sonnet-5-5-review` (Claude Sonnet 5.5, Anthropic), the same vendor and another model than the implementer (Claude Opus 5.5, session `f2a4d9f2-d557-4064-ae96-6ed52bb47cd2`); by the rule of 2026-10-09 another model satisfies `different-actor`, another vendor would be preferred. Rounds 1 and 2 were Codex (OpenAI), round 3 and this one Claude Sonnet 5.5. N02 (a detached run read a relative `--note-file` from the project root instead of the caller's directory) and the follow-ups F-R3-01, F-R3-03, F-R3-04, F-R3-06, F-R3-08 and F-R3-10 were reconciled as fixed; no blocker remains.\n\n## Current state\n\nOpen, all owned by @oseiaspereira88 with review on 2026-11-25:\n\n- F01 (low, disclosed under D7): the live view redacts with what the stream has declared so far, and the record is derived again from the whole transcript; a capture the record later withholds can still have been shown live.\n- F-R3-02 (low, graded medium in round 3 and lowered because the hand-back now precedes the agent, which leaves a window of about 100ms or a supervisor hung over 30s; the engine accepts only low as accepted risk): the SIGTERM, 30s grace and SIGKILL path of a stopped supervisor has no test (swapping SIGTERM for SIGKILL keeps the delegate suites green), and the SIGKILL fallback on the supervisor group does not reach the agent's own group.\n- F-R3-05 (low): after `kill -9` of the supervisor the run is `stalled` at about 35s, but its agent keeps running and the live file, claim and marker stay.\n- F-R3-07 (low): `on_terminal` fires for `pose delegate review|resume`, not for the deprecated alias `pose review dispatch --via … --apply` nor for a run recorded `stalled`.\n- F-R3-09 (low): a reader racing back-to-back heartbeat rewrites can read \"ledger file changed while opening\" and list the marker as a loss (8 of 8 under 6 readers and 400 consecutive beats; 0 in 115k reads at a beat every 10ms; the real beat is every 10s).\n- F-R4-01 (low): F-R3-03, F-R3-04, F-R3-08 and F-R3-10 have no regression test; reverting each leaves `Delegat|Watch|Stall|Help|Detach` green. F-R3-04 and F-R3-10 are testable.\n- F-R4-02 (low): since F-R3-01 a hook claimed less than six minutes ago reads as no hook, so an outcome that could not be published is invisible to `ls` for up to six minutes before it is reported as lost; the foreground command still prints the failure it had.\n- F-R4-03 (low): the four manuals do not say how a relative `on_terminal` command is resolved (a name with a separator is a path from the project root, a bare name goes through PATH, `..` leaves the project).\n- F-R4-04 (low): `watch` of a `stalled` run prints only the stall marker, not the events its live file still holds, and the paths `--detach` prints are project-root-relative although the run now starts in the caller's directory.\n- F-R4-05 (low): the regression tests of the review rounds are named outside the `TestDelegat*`/`TestDelegate*` family (`TestADetachedRunReadsTheCallersFiles`, `TestHookBoundAndRelativeCommand`, `TestAnUnrecordedHookOutcomeIsReported`, `TestADetachedRunThatCannotHandBackDoesNotRun`), so the `delegation-surface-integration` check and the documented race selection do not run them; only the unit lane does.\n\n## Next checks\n\n`go test ./internal/pose ./internal/cli -run 'TestADetachedRun|TestHookBound|TestAnUnrecordedHook' -count=1` and the race selection extended with those names. For F-R3-02 a test that holds a supervisor past the grace. For F-R4-01 a stalled-watch test and a test whose live file cannot be read. Run `pose delegate --help` and the four manuals after any change.\n\n## Risks\n\nNone of these changes dispatch, ledger, continuity, provenance or containment; each is either a disclosure gap, a test gap or a window of at most six minutes. F-R3-05 and F-R3-02 can leave an agent running after its supervisor died; the stalled state and `ls` name the run, and `pose delegate cancel` stops one that is still recorded.\n\n## Next owner\n\n@oseiaspereira88, review 2026-11-25. No upstream contribution draft or issue was created.\n\n## References\n\nSpec: `.pose/specs/2026-10-18-pose-delegated-review-completion-signal.md`. ADR: `.pose/adr/2026-10-26-delegation-is-a-first-class-execution-surface.md`. Roadmap: `.pose/roadmaps/delegation.md`. Review report: `.review-work/round4/REPORT.md` (local evidence only).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
